#{[17592186120314 :question/problem "The matrix $C$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b ∈ Col(C)$.\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2} \\cdot x_{3}$, where $x_{1}, x_{2}$ and $x_{3}$ are real numbers such that $b = x_{1}c_{1} + x_{2}c_{2} + x_{3}c_{3}$, and $c_{1}$, $c_{2}$, and $c_{3}$ are the respective columns of $C$.\n\n$C = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186120316 :question/problem "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s$. Find the curvature at the point where $s = \\frac{5π}{2}$ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n$T(s) = ⟨\\frac{3}{5},-\\frac{4}{5}\\sin \\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right), \\frac{4}{5}\\cos \\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)⟩$."] [17592186120320 :question/problem "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]?$"] [17592186120321 :question/problem "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [17592186120323 :question/problem "The matrix $A$ and vectors $b_{1}$, $b_{2}$, $b_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $Null(A)$?\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}, b_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, b_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, b_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186120329 :question/problem "![](images/d3/d399a25c2a73883560aade5946007b21bf0b853e5d3ca262469df9b75e5843cd.png)\n\nConsider the RCL circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n$q(t) = Ae^{-5t/2} + Be^{-t} + \\frac{P}{41}\\cos (2t) + \\frac{Q}{82}\\sin (2t)$\nwhere $A$, $B$, $P$, and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P + Q$.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*"] [17592186120330 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $(39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-65\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $70\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.01\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $10\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=70\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=70I=70\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.01}=100q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=10\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=10\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dq}{dt}\\right)=10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-65\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=70\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q+10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+7\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{39}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+7\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+10{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+B{e}^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+7(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\, &  \\\\ +10(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{39}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-21\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(\\alpha +21\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{39}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-21\\alpha +\\beta &=\\frac{39}{10}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ \\alpha +21\\beta &=-\\frac{13}{2}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{5},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{3}{10}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+B{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+B{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{3}{10}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.36,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [17592186120332 :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n$q(t) = 3e^{-2t} - e^{-4t} + \\frac{4}{13}\\cos (3t) - \\frac{3}{13}\\sin (3t)$.\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes."] [17592186120334 :question/problem "The matrix $A$ and vectors $b_{1}$, $b_{2}$, $b_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $Null(A)$?\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, b_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}, b_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, b_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186120335 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $105\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{180}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $15\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=105\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=105I=105\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/180)}=180q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=15\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=15\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=15\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dq}{dt}\\right)=15\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=105\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+180q+15\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+7\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+12q=\\frac{10}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+7\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+12{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+7(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\, &  \\\\ +12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{10}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-21\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(3\\alpha +21\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{10}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-21\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 3\\alpha +21\\beta &=\\frac{10}{3}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{45},$ $\\beta =\\frac{7}{45}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{45}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{45}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{1}{45}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{45}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{1}{45}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{45}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-4A{e}^{-4t}-3B{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-4A{e}^{-4t}-3B{e}^{-3t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{15}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{7}{15}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.47,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [17592186120336 :question/problem "The matrices $A$ and $B$ are given below. Given that $A$ is row equivalent to $B$, then find a basis of $Null(A)$.\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186120340 :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit is governed by the differential equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}q}{dt^{2}} + 5\\frac{dq}{dt} + 6q = 12$.\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs."] [17592186120345 :question/problem "The curvature of the plane curve $r(t) =-t^{2}i + 2t^{3}j$ is given by\n$κ ( t ) = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2 | t | \\cdot  \\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A A A A A A}}}} , \\hspace{1em} t \\ne  0.$\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?"] [17592186120351 :question/problem "Find the value of $x_{1} + x_{2}$ given that $2a - 6x = b$ and\n$a = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -22 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186120353 :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)$ at the point $r(1)$ if the curve and the derivative $T^{′}(t)$ of the corresponding unit tangent vector are given by\n$r(t) =-6ti + 3t^{2}j + t^{3}k, T^{′}(t) = (1)/((t^{2} + 2)^{2})(4ti + (4 - 2t^{2})j + 4tk)$."] [17592186120357 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$, where\n$b_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}, b_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, b_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix}$.\nIf $[x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of $x_{1} + x_{2} + x_{3}$?"] [17592186120365 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\}.$"] [17592186120367 :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}})=$"] [17592186120367 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}})=1.$"] [17592186120373 :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ What is $(2u)\\cdot v?$"] [17592186120378 :question/problem "The signed curvature of the plane curve $r(t) = ⟨\\frac{1}{t}, t⟩$ is given by\n$κ ( t ) = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A A}}}{\\sqrt{( 1 + {t}^{4} {)}^{3}}}} , \\hspace{1em} t \\ne  0.$\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?"] [17592186120380 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$, where\n$b_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, b_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix}$.\nIf $[x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of $x_{1} + x_{2}$?"] [17592186120382 :question/problem "Find the vector $x$, if $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$ is a basis of $Span{b_{1}, b_{2}}$, and\n$b_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}, b_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, [x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186120384 :question/problem "Which of the following matrices have a rank of $4$?\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 5 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186120385 :question/problem "If \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$, find $\\text{rank}(A).$"] [17592186120394 :question/problem "Given that  $\\text{Null}(A)={\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{3}+2{x}_{4} \\\\ 2{x}_{3}-5{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,{x}_{3},{x}_{4}\\in R}$, find a basis for $\\text{Null}(A).$"] [17592186120400 :question/problem "Find the cofactor of ${a}_{23}$ in \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -7 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 11 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186120401 :question/problem "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186120403 :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}|=$"] [17592186120408 :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\frac{1}{x - 1} - \\frac{1}{x + 4}$."] [17592186120419 :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{11}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*"] [17592186120423 :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 9 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}|=$"] [17592186120424 :question/problem "Calculate\n$|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|.$"] [17592186120425 :question/problem "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $2$nd row:\n$\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}$."] [17592186120429 :question/problem "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 1 & 19 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186120433 :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}5 & -8 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -7 & 5 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{33}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*"] [17592186120434 :question/problem "$\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & -7 & 10 \\\\ -9 & -1 & -6 & 9 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 3 & -11 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & -7\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nConsider the above $4 \\cdot 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3 \\cdot 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above $a_{42}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186120435 :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+3 & -5 \\\\ b & 6\\end{aligned}|=-7$,\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+2 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{aligned}|.$"] [17592186120439 :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =20-2 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & k \\\\ -2 & -12\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}5 & k \\\\ -2 & -12\\end{aligned}|}{18} \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5 & k \\\\ -2 & -12\\end{aligned}| & =-54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $|\\begin{aligned}5 & k \\\\ -2 & -12\\end{aligned}|=-54$ only."] [17592186120444 :question/problem "What is $\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 & \\sqrt{2} & 0.5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}$?"] [17592186120445 :question/problem "Find the determinant of the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186120446 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,7x-2y &=10 \\\\ -2x+y &=8.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as \n$x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{|\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|}$, where ${D}_{x}=$"] [17592186120447 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6x+8y &=-46 \\\\ 6x+5y &=20.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $y$ can be written as \n$y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}|},$ \nwhere ${D}_{y}=$"] [17592186120448 :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}|} \\\\ 3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|$ only."] [17592186120449 :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},$ find $det(A).$"] [17592186120450 :question/problem "Given that $A = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$, find $det(A)$."] [17592186120457 :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 100\\end{aligned}|=$"] [17592186120470 :question/problem "What is the $4$th term of the geometric sequence given below?\n$a_{n + 1} =-2a_{n}, a_{1} = 1, n \\ge 1$"] [17592186120472 :question/problem "What is the $3$rd term of the geometric sequence given below?\n$f(n + 1) = \\frac{2}{3}f(n), f(1) =-36, n \\ge 1$"] [17592186120473 :question/problem "What is the $5$th term of the geometric sequence given below?\n\n$f(n + 1) =-2f(n), f(1) =-5, n \\ge 1$"] [17592186120492 :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot p(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,p(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)p\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right){p}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-\\,j+2k)+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+\\,j) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(i+k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}(i+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n $T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+k).$"] [17592186120505 :question/problem "Find the cofactor of $d_{33}$ in $D = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 9 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 6 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186120509 :question/problem "What is the minor of ${x}_{33}$ in \n$X=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [17592186120510 :question/problem "Find the minor of ${q}_{23}$ in \n$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 9 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186120519 :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 10\\end{bmatrix},$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$"] [17592186120521 :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n$T(t) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}} \\cdot q(t)$.\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t = 3$, if\n$q(3) = ⟨3, 1⟩, q^{′}(3) = ⟨1, 0⟩$."] [17592186120523 :question/problem "The vectors $a_{1}$, $a_{2}$, and $a_{3}$ are given below. Determine whether $B = {a_{1}, a_{2}}$ is a basis of $Span{a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}}$. If that is the case, find $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$, where $a_{3} = x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2}$.\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186120526 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$, where\n$b_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, b_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, b_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nIf $[x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of $x_{1}$?"] [17592186120529 :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=$"] [17592186120529 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.$"] [17592186120531 :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(\\sqrt{2}, 2, 1)$ where $t = 1$, given that\n\n$T(1) = \\frac{1}{3}(i + 2\\sqrt{2}j), N(1) = k$.\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively."] [17592186120554 :question/problem "Calculate\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}[2f(t) - g(t)]dt$\ngiven that\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}f(t)dt = ⟨1,-1, 1⟩$\nand\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}g(t)dt = ⟨ - 3,-2, 1⟩$."] [17592186120555 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,4⟩$\nand\n${\\int }_{2}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨8,\\,-6,\\,15⟩.$"] [17592186120556 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)+⟨1,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,4⟩.$"] [17592186120557 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{1}2f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨1,\\,-3,\\,2⟩.$"] [17592186120983 :tutorial/content "In many cases, the EMF produced by a power source is sinusoidal, not constant. Such a power source is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/43/4398148f91eda2bae1392f79e868e10c8967ecc23ca9f26b791bc1fbacc45ff9.png)\n\nIn a first-order linear RL circuit driven by a sinusoidal voltage, the solution consists of a transient current that decays over time and a steady-state current that oscillates with the same frequency as the input and is independent of the initial condition.\n\nFor example, let's find the amplitude of the steady-state current in the RL circuit shown below.\n\n![](images/59/59fd8a8db1a227a578670d2457011275446c87ba218686f8d1984f4a73a8cfa6.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\sin(4t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $7\\,\\Omega,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 7\\,\\Omega.$\n\n$$\nV_R = 7I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\n$$\nV_L = 1\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3\\sin(4t) = 7I + 1\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 7I = 3\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 7I_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nI_c(t) = Ae^{-7t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(4t) + \\beta\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+7(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(7\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +7\\beta &=3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 7\\alpha +4\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = -\\dfrac{12}{65},$ $\\beta = \\dfrac{21}{65}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = -\\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-7t} - \\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = Ae^{-7t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $I_p(t) =  - \\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t) \\approx I_p(t) = -\\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{12}{65}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{21}{65}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.37\\,\\textrm{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places. This amplitude represents the maximum magnitude of the steady-state current flowing through the circuit."] [17592186121001 :tutorial/content "Consider the curve $C$ parametrized by the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 2\\sin t, \\: 2\\cos t, \\: \\sqrt{5} t\\rangle, \\qquad t \\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nFor any $t >0,$ the arc length $s$ of $C$ from $\\mathbf r(0)$ to $\\mathbf r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,\\sqrt{5}⟩‖\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡u+{sin}^{2}⁡u)+5}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{9}\\,du \\\\  & =3u{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $s= 3t,$ or equivalently $t = \\dfrac{s}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we can define a new vector function $\\mathbf{R}$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(s)= \\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right) = \\left\\langle 2\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right), \\: 2\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right), \\: \\sqrt{5} \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right) \\right\\rangle .\n$$\n\nThe function $\\mathbf{R}(s)$ gives us the *same* curve $C,$ but this time it is parameterized by the arc length $s.$\n\nIn general, if $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$ is a continuously differentiable vector function such that $\\mathbf r'(t )\\neq \\mathbf{0},$ then to parametrize it in terms of arc length we perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Compute $s = \\displaystyle s(t) = \\int_a^t\\|\\mathbf r'(u)\\| \\, \\mathrm{d}u$\n\n2\\. Write $s = s(t)$ in the form $t=t(s)$\n\n3\\. Substitute $t=t(s)$ back into $\\mathbf r(t)$ to get $\\mathbf R(s)=\\mathbf r(t(s))$\n\n**Note:** One advantage of parametrizing a curve by its arc length is that the tangent vector is always a unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\left\\|\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf R}{\\textrm{d} s}\\right\\| = 1\n$$"] [17592186121015 :tutorial/content "We can use the unit circle to find the sine and cosine of the special *negative* angles. We do this by first finding a coterminal special angle that lies in the range $[0^\\circ , 360^\\circ ),$ and then using $x = \\cos(\\theta)$ and $y = \\sin(\\theta)$ as we did before. \n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFor example, to find $\\cos(-225^\\circ ),$ first, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-225^\\circ$ that lies in the range $[0,360^\\circ).$\n\n$$\n-225^\\circ + 360^\\circ = 135^\\circ\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\cos\\left(-225^\\circ\\right) = \\cos 135^\\circ.$\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $135^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $\\left( -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\cos 135^\\circ  = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\cos \\left(-225^\\circ\\right) =-\\dfrac {\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"] [17592186121016 :tutorial/content "We can also extend our knowledge of special trigonometric ratios to angles greater than $360^\\circ.$ Just like for negative numbers, we find a coterminal special angle that lies in the range $[0^\\circ , 360^\\circ )$, then use $x = \\cos(\\theta)$ and $y = \\sin(\\theta)$ as before.\n\nFor example, to find $\\sin 420^\\circ$, first we find an angle that's coterminal with $420^\\circ$ that lies in the range $[0,360^\\circ).$\n\n$$\n420^\\circ - 360^\\circ = 60^\\circ\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sin\\left(420^\\circ\\right) = \\sin 60^\\circ.$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $60^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $\\left( \\dfrac{1}{2}, \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin 60^\\circ  = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\sin \\left(420^\\circ\\right) =\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$"] [17592186121026 :tutorial/content "Cramer's rule applies to square systems of linear equations with any number of variables. In this lesson, we will practice applying Cramer's rule to solve $3 \\times 3$ systems of equations.\n\nSuppose we have a square system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y+{a}_{13}z &={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y+{a}_{23}z &={b}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{31}x+{a}_{32}y+{a}_{33}z &={b}_{3},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $D={\\left| \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{blue}{a}_{11}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{12}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{13}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{a}_{21}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{22}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{23}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{a}_{31}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{32}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{33}}\\end{array} \\right|} \\neq 0$. \nThen Cramer's rule states that\n\n$$\n\\left< x,y,z \\right> = \\left\\langle  \\dfrac{D_x}{D}, \\dfrac{D_y}{D}, \\dfrac{D_z}{D} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nis the unique solution of the system, where\n\n$$\nD_x={\\left| \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{red}{b}_{1}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{12}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{13}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{b}_{2}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{22}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{23}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{b}_{3}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{32}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{33}}\\end{array} \\right|},\n\t\\qquad\n\tD_y={\\left| \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{blue}{a}_{11}} & {\\color{red}{b}_{1}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{13}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{a}_{21}} & {\\color{red}{b}_{2}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{23}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{a}_{31}} & {\\color{red}{b}_{3}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{33}}\\end{array} \\right|},\n\t\\qquad\n\tD_z={\\left| \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{blue}{a}_{11}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{12}} & {\\color{red}{b}_{1}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{a}_{21}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{22}} & {\\color{red}{b}_{2}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{a}_{31}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{32}} & {\\color{red}{b}_{3}}\\end{array} \\right|}.\n$$"] [17592186121029 :tutorial/content "Recall that LU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- $L = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ ∗ & {\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & {\\color{blue}1}\\end{array} \\right]}$ is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- $U = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} & ∗\\end{array} \\right]}$ is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the matrix $A = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & - 1 & - 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & - 3 \\\\ - 2 & 2 & 7\\end{array} \\right]}.$ To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & - 1 & - 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & - 3 \\\\ - 2 & 2 & 7\\end{array} \\right]}\n}_{A}\n=\n\\underbrace{\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ ∗ & {\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & {\\color{blue}1}\\end{array} \\right]}\n}_{L}\n\\cdot\n\\underbrace{\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} & ∗\\end{array} \\right]}\n}_{U}.\n$$\n\nAs usual, to find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we can reduce the matrix $A$ to *echelon form* while simultaneously performing the same elementary row operations on the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⇔ & \\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]=U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** In order to get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to its *echelon form*:\n\n- we can only add multiples of higher rows to lower rows ($R_j:=R_j+k R_i$ where $i < j$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed."] [17592186121110 :tutorial/content "Let's prove the results using this lesson by considering all possible cases for products of even and odd functions:\n\n- Case I: Let $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be non-zero even functions. Then, we have that\n$f(-x) = f(x),\\qquad g(-x) = g(x)$.\nNow define $h(x) = f(x)\\cdot g(x)$. Therefore,\n$h(-x)|= f(-x) \\cdot g(-x); = f(x) \\cdot g(x); = h(x)$.\nSince $h(x)$ is even, we conclude that $f(x)\\cdot g(x)$ is even.\n- Case II: Let $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be non-zero odd functions. Then, we have that\n$f(-x) = -f(x),\\qquad g(-x) = -g(x)$.\nNow define $h(x) = f(x)\\cdot g(x)$. Therefore,\n$h(-x)|= f(-x) \\cdot g(-x); = (-f(x)) \\cdot (-g(x)); = f(x) \\cdot g(x); = h(x)$.\nSince $h(x)$ is even, we conclude that $f(x)\\cdot g(x)$ is even.\n- Case III: Let $f(x)$ be non-zero even and $g(x)$ be non-zero odd. Then, we have that\n$f(-x) = f(x),\\qquad g(-x) = -g(x)$.\nNow define $h(x) = f(x)\\cdot g(x)$. Therefore,\n$h(-x)|= f(-x) \\cdot g(-x); = f(x) \\cdot (-g(x)); = - (f(x) \\cdot g(x)); =-h(x)$.\nSince $h(x)$ is odd, we conclude that $f(x)\\cdot g(x)$ is odd.\n- Case IV: Let $f(x)$ be non-zero odd and $g(x)$ be non-zero even. Then, we have that\n$f(-x) = -f(x),\\qquad g(-x) = g(x)$.\nNow define $h(x) = f(x)\\cdot g(x)$. Therefore,\n$h(-x)|= f(-x) \\cdot g(-x); =-f(x) \\cdot g(x); = - (f(x) \\cdot g(x)); =-h(x)$.\nSince $h(x)$ is odd, we conclude that $f(x)\\cdot g(x)$ is odd."] [17592186124193 :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find  $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} f(x).$ \n\n![](images/c7/c7ee917a93ed51c189979516b9ab0d3232c183921ee277f522f617518ce03fdf.png)"] [17592186124212 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\dfrac {x^2} {4} +  \\dfrac {y^2} 9  + z^2 = 1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$"] [17592186124213 :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet  $\\dfrac{(x-3)^2}9 + \\dfrac{(y+4)^2}{16} - \\dfrac{(z-5)^2}{25} = 1,$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis."] [17592186124214 :question/problem "Identify the surface $z -1 = \\dfrac{(x-3)^2}{4} - \\dfrac{(y+2)^2}4.$"] [17592186124217 :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $\\mathbf{a}\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(\\mathbf a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) <                0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) >0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) <       0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since $𝒇'(x,y) = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 - 2 x & 2 + 2 y\\end{array} \\right]},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 2-2x, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =2+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2-2x)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2+2y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2+2y)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of $\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,-1) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1)) = -2 \\cdot 2 - 0^2 = -4 < 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,-1) = (\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1))_{11} = -2 \\lt 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(1,-1) < 0,$ the point $(1,-1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186124218 :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occurs at its critical points. A point $\\mathbf a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${𝒇}^{\\prime } ( \\mathbf{a} ) = {\\mathbf{0}}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${𝒇}^{\\prime } ( \\mathbf{a} )$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $\\mathbf{a}\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(\\mathbf a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf a) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf a$\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) >0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf a$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf a) < 0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf a$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${𝒇}^{\\prime } ( x , y ) = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}3 {x}^{2} - 3 & 2 y\\end{array} \\right] ,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 3x^2 - 3, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =2y.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${𝒇}^{\\prime } ( x , y ) = {\\mathbf{0}}^{T} ,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 {x}^{2} - 3 = 0 \\\\ 2 y = 0\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n3x^2 -3= 0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n2y = 0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\ny=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,0)$ and $(-1,0)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rlrl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  {x}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  x}} ( 3 {x}^{2} - 3 ) = 6 x \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  x \\partial  y}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  x}} ( 2 y ) = 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  y \\partial  x}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  y}} ( 3 {x}^{2} - 3 ) = 0 \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  {y}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  y}} ( 2 y ) = 2\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}{𝒇}^{\\prime \\prime } ( x , y ) &  = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\frac{{\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  {x}^{2}} & \\frac{{\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  x \\partial  y} \\\\ \\frac{{\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  y \\partial  x} & \\frac{{\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  {y}^{2}}\\end{array} \\right] = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}6 x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right] .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c}{𝒇}^{\\prime \\prime } ( 1 , 0 ) = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right] .\\end{array}\n$$\nComputing the determinant $H(1,0),$ we get\n$$\nH ( 1 , 0 ) = \\det  ( {𝒇}^{\\prime \\prime } ( 1 , 0 ) ) = 6 \\cdot  2 - {0}^{2} = 12 > 0.\n$$\nNow, since $H(1,0) > 0$ and  $\\frac{{\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  {x}^{2}} ( 1 , 0 ) = ( {𝒇}^{\\prime \\prime } ( 1 , 0 ) {)}_{11} = 6 > 0 ,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(1,0).$\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-1,0),$ we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c}{𝒇}^{\\prime \\prime } ( - 1 , 0 ) = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}- 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right] .\\end{array}\n$$\nComputing the determinant $H(-1,0),$ we get\n$$\nH ( - 1 , 0 ) = \\det  ( {𝒇}^{\\prime \\prime } ( - 1 , 0 ) ) = - 6 \\cdot  2 - {0}^{2} = - 12 < 0\n$$\nTherefore, $f(-1,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$ and a saddle point at $(-1,0).$"] [17592186124231 :question/problem "Solve the following recurrence relation for $n\\geq 1$.\n$a_n - 6a_{n-1} = 0, \\qquad a_1=7, \\quad n \\geq 2$."] [17592186124232 :question/problem "Solve the recurrence relation\n$a_n = a_{n-1} + 6\\cdot 7^n, \\qquad a_1=8, \\quad n \\geq 2$."] [17592186124233 :question/problem "Solve the recurrence relation\n$a_n = a_{n-1} + 3n^2 - n + 1, \\qquad a_1= 3, \\quad n \\geq 2$."] [17592186124238 :question/problem "The figure below shows the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$. Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\,-2} \\Bigl(3f(x) - g(x)\\Bigr)$.\n\n![](images/43/439ebf5a1e5812c6fb27da2dd4b3015ea757133343b86695ebad44db6eaeae7c.png)"] [17592186124243 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_x =  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2} \\pi}{4} \\sin{\\left(2 x + \\dfrac{\\pi y}{4} \\right)},$ find a tangent vector to $C$ at $P.$"] [17592186124244 :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac {\\partial^2 f }{\\partial x\\partial y}$ given that $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} =  \\dfrac {2y} {x + y^2}.$"] [17592186124256 :question/problem "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,2)}} \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}.$"] [17592186124257 :question/problem "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2}.$"] [17592186124261 :question/problem "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2}.$"] [17592186124264 :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\\!\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2x},\\,\\frac{3}{2y}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)},\\frac{3}{2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction of $\\nabla g\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})‖ & =‖⟨1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124266 :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16} - 1,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(0,4).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,4) & =\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(4)}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4).$\n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle v_x, v_y \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{x-{x}_{0}}=\\frac{{v}_{y}}{{v}_{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{y}(x-{x}_{0}) & ={v}_{x}(y-{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(x-0) & =0(y-4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the equation of the normal line to the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1$ at the point $(0,4)$ is $x=0.$"] [17592186124268 :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy) \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z) = \\ln (x^2 - xy) - z,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2){:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)k \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{2x-y}{{x}^{2}-xy}\\right)\\text{ }i+\\left(\\frac{1}{y-x}\\right)\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =\\left(\\frac{2\\cdot 2-1}{{2}^{2}-2\\cdot 1}\\right)\\text{ }i+\\left(\\frac{1}{1-2}\\right)\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}i-\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf v = \\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2)= \\left\\langle  \\dfrac {3} {2}, -1, -1\\right  \\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}=\\frac{y-1}{-1}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124271 :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find the limit $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).$ \n\n![](images/49/4982eb6e9020e5f6235049fd36a030a69d7b74a0e3ea60323ac8887f4c9bca79.png)"] [17592186124272 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\,1} \\left(6x^2+x\\right)$."] [17592186124273 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits {x\\to (\\pi/4)} 2\\sec \\left(2x\\right)$."] [17592186124274 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits {x \\to \\pi} \\csc x$"] [17592186124275 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits {x \\to \\pi/3} \\cot \\left(3x\\right)$."] [17592186124286 :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} \\, .$"] [17592186124289 :question/problem "Calculate  $\\lim_\\limits {x\\rightarrow -1} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} .$"] [17592186124302 :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\bigg\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & - 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right]} \\bigg\\}$ is a linearly independent set in $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$\n\nII. $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\bigg\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & - 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right]} \\bigg\\}$\n\nIII. $\\bigg\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & - 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right]} \\bigg\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$"] [17592186124307 :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\bigg\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 7\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 4 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} \\bigg\\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\bigg\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 2 \\\\ - 5\\end{array} \\right]} \\bigg\\}$ be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$"] [17592186124311 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\dfrac {(x-2)^2} {2} +  {y^2} + \\dfrac{(z+1)^2}{2} =1,$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis."] [17592186124319 :question/problem "Given $A={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}2 & 1 & 3 & - 2 \\\\ 0 & - 3 & - 2 & - 1 \\\\ - 6 & 0 & - 7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & 3\\end{array} \\right]},\\;$ what is $\\textrm{nullity}(A)?$"] [17592186124325 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]}\\longrightarrow\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 x + 4 y \\\\ 3 x + 6 y\\end{array} \\right]}.$ Find $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}).$"] [17592186124326 :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{array} \\right]}\\longrightarrow\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 x + 4 y \\\\ 4 x + 10 y - z \\\\ z - 6 x - 12 y\\end{array} \\right]},$ find $\\,\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}).$"] [17592186124327 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{array} \\right]}\n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 x + 12 y - 3 z \\\\ - 2 x + 4 y - 2 z \\\\ 6 x - 3 y + 3 z\\end{array} \\right]}$ and the vector $\\,\\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}k \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{array} \\right]}.$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,\\mathbf{u}\\in\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}).$"] [17592186124329 :question/problem "If $T={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}2 & - 3 & - 1 & - 2 \\\\ - 6 & 5 & - 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ then what is $\\text{rank}(\\mathbf{T})?$"] [17592186124330 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ into $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Im} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{3} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 3 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 0\\end{array}} \\right.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186124331 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿$A - \\lambda I = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}- 9 - \\lambda  & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 9 - \\lambda  & - 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 - \\lambda \\end{array} \\right]}$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0.$ \n\nSince the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the entries on the main diagonal, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-9-\\lambda  & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 9-\\lambda  & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )(9-\\lambda )(6-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +9)(\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=-9,$ $\\lambda_2=9,$ and $\\lambda_3=6.$ \n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are always the entries on the main diagonal."] [17592186124332 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $A-\\lambda I{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿$A - \\lambda I = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 - \\lambda  & - 1 & - 2 \\\\ - 1 & 1 - \\lambda  & - 2 \\\\ - 2 & - 2 & - 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} \\right]}$\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\det  ( A - \\lambda  I ) &  = 0 \\\\ ∣ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 - \\lambda  & - 1 & - 2 \\\\ - 1 & 1 - \\lambda  & - 2 \\\\ - 2 & - 2 & - 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} ∣ &  = 0 \\\\ ( 1 - \\lambda  ) ∣ \\begin{array}{cc}1 - \\lambda  & - 2 \\\\ - 2 & - 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} ∣ - ( - 1 ) ∣ \\begin{array}{cc}- 1 & - 2 \\\\ - 2 & - 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} ∣ + ( - 2 ) ∣ \\begin{array}{cc}- 1 & 1 - \\lambda  \\\\ - 2 & - 2\\end{array} ∣ &  = 0 \\\\ ( - {\\lambda }^{3} - 5 {\\lambda }^{2} + 17 \\lambda  - 11 ) + ( 3 + \\lambda  ) - 2 ( 4 - 2 \\lambda  ) &  = 0 \\\\ - {\\lambda }^{3} - 5 {\\lambda }^{2} + 22 \\lambda  - 16 &  = 0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3} + 5 {\\lambda }^{2} - 22 \\lambda  + 16 &  = 0\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of $16\\mathbin{:}$ \n$$\n\\pm1, \\pm2, \\pm4, \\pm8, \\pm 16\n$$\n\nLet's pick $\\lambda=1.$ Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle ( 1 {)}^{3} + 5 ( 1 {)}^{2} - 22 ( 1 ) + 16 & \\displaystyle  = 0.\\end{array}\n$$\nSo, $\\lambda=1$ is a root of the equation. This means we can factor $(\\lambda-1)$ out of $\\lambda^3 +5 \\lambda^2 - 22 \\lambda + 16$ using synthetic division:\n\n| $1$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-22$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $6$ | $-16$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $-16$ | $0$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(λ - 1)(λ^{2} + 6λ - 16) &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)(λ - 2)(λ + 8) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=1,$ $\\lambda_2=2,$ and $\\,\\lambda_3=-8.$"] [17592186124338 :question/worked-solution "Given a $4 \\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\qquad \\Leftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Col} ( M ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{4} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{dim(Col} ( M ) ) = 4 = \\text{rank} ( M ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Null} ( M ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{dim(Null} ( M ) ) = 0 = \\text{nullity} ( M )\\end{array}} \\right.$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(M) + \\textrm{nullity}(M) = 4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B+4I) & =4-\\text{rank}(B+4I) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) \\ne \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{nullity}(B+4I) \\neq 0,$ we obtain that $B +4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B +4 I$ is singular, then $(B +4 I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda=-4$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186124343 :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are  \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=-2, \\quad$ $\\lambda_2=-1, \\quad$ $\\lambda_3=2.$ \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_1=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 14\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{8}{3}\\right){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_2=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=x_3.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{array} \\right]},\n\t\\quad x_3 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $x_3=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $P = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{1} & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{0} & 3 & 2 \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{1} & 2 & 3\\end{array} \\right]}.$"] [17592186124356 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{array} \\right]} \\longrightarrow {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x + y \\\\ - x + y \\\\ 2 y\\end{array} \\right]}$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $\\mathbf{v}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ k \\\\ 8\\end{array} \\right]}$ belongs to $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}).$"] [17592186124367 :question/problem "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2-4y}{x-2\\sqrt{y}}.$"] [17592186124380 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, P-\\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿$P - \\lambda I = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}3 - \\lambda  & 0 & - 8 & - 2 \\\\ 0 & - 1 - \\lambda  & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 - \\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & - 3 & 1 - \\lambda \\end{array} \\right]}$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $\\det(P-\\lambda I) = 0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4 \\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 0 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda  & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 7 & -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3 \\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-\\lambda )\\cdot (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 7 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-35) & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-36) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -6)(\\lambda +6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda=3,$ $\\lambda=4,$ $\\lambda=6,$ and $\\lambda=-6$ (all with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186124386 :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{4x^4}.$"] [17592186124387 :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{(1-x)^4} \\, .$"] [17592186124388 :question/worked-solution "The side-side-side (SSS) similarity criterion states the following:\n\n> Two triangles are similar if the ratios of the lengths of their corresponding sides are equal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given triangles.\n\n- $\\mathcal{P}$ is similar to $\\mathcal{T}$ by SSS since the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are equal:\n$\\dfrac{16}{8}=\\dfrac{12}{6} =\\dfrac{8}{4}= 2$\n\n- $\\mathcal{Q}$ is *not* similar to $\\mathcal{T}$ by SSS since the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are not equal:\n$\\dfrac{12}{8}\\neq\\dfrac{10}{6}\\neq\\dfrac{8}{4}$.\n- $\\mathcal{R}$ is *not* simlar to $\\mathcal{T}$ by SSS since the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are not equal:\n$\\dfrac{12}{8}\\neq\\dfrac{10}{6}\\neq\\dfrac{9}{4}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathcal{P}$ only.\""] [17592186124392 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty} \\dfrac{3x^4+9x}{6x^4-x^3}.$"] [17592186124394 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -\\infty} \\dfrac{4-5x-2x^3}{x^2+3}.$"] [17592186124395 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{3}-15x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{15x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{15}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+0}{1-0} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$"] [17592186124396 :question/problem "Given the elliptic cone $\\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{4} + \\dfrac{(y-2)^2}{2} = \\dfrac{(z+1)^2}{3},$ find its intercepts with the $z$-axis."] [17592186124399 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $\\dfrac {x^2} 2 +  \\dfrac {(y-2)^2}4  = 1?$"] [17592186124408 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{5}-10}{{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{10}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{10}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{8}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}-0}{1-0}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [17592186124417 :question/worked-solution "Given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\qquad \\Leftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Col} ( M ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{3} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{dim(Col} ( M ) ) = 3 = \\text{rank} ( M ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Null} ( M ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{dim(Null} ( M ) ) = 0 = \\text{nullity} ( M )\\end{array}} \\right.$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M = A- 7I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ are eigenvectors of $A,$ which are nonzero by definition. As a result, the matrix $M=A-7 I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) \\neq 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186124419 :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y, z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c$ such that\n\n$$\nf (\\mathbf b) − f (\\mathbf a) = \\nabla f (\\mathbf c) · (\\mathbf b − \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x, y,z) = x^2 + y - z^2$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,0,1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b= \\langle 2,3,0\\rangle$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,3,0)-f(0,0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,3,0⟩-⟨0,0,1⟩) \\\\ 7-(-1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ 8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\nabla f$ and evaluating at $\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1, c_2, c_3\\rangle,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,1,-2z⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1},1,-2{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ 8 & =⟨2{c}_{1},1,-2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ 8 & =4{c}_{1}+3+2{c}_{3} \\\\ 5 & =4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{y}{3}=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx = 2t , \\quad y= 3t, \\quad z = 1-t, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf c= \\langle c_1, c_2, c_3\\rangle$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\nc_1 = 2t , \\quad c_2 = 3t , \\quad c_3 = 1-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $4c_1 + 2c_3 = 5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2t)+2(1-t) & =5 \\\\ 8t+2-2t & =5 \\\\ 6t & =3 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of $c_1,$ $c_2,$ and $c_3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=1, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $\\left(1, \\dfrac{3}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$"] [17592186124430 :question/problem "Solve the recurrence relation\n\n$a_n = 8a_{n-1} - 16a_{n-2}, \\quad a_0 = 1, \\quad a_1 = -8$."] [17592186124430 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\na_n=\\lambda^n\n$$\n\nThen, we have that\n\n$$\na_{n-1} = \\lambda^{n-1}, \\qquad a_{n-2} = \\lambda^{n-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our relation gives\n\n$$\n\\lambda^n = 8\\lambda^{n-1} - 16\\lambda^{n-2}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{n} - 8λ^{n - 1} + 16λ^{n - 2} &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{n - 2}(λ^{2} - 8λ + 16) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 8\\lambda + 16 = 0\n$$\n\nFactoring, we get\n\n$$\n(\\lambda - 4)^2 = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda = 4$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo, the general solution to the recurrence relation is\n\n$$\na_n = A \\cdot 4^n + Bn \\cdot 4^n\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions:\n\n- Substituting $a_0 = 1$ into the general solution gives\n$A = 1$.\n- Substituting $a_1 = -8$ into the general solution gives\n$-8|= 4A + 4B; - 2|= A + B$.\nUsing the fact that $A=1$, we get\n$-2|= 1 + B; B|=-3$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the recurrence relation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 1 \\cdot 4^{n} - 3n \\cdot 4^{n} \\\\\n&= 4^{n} - 3n \\cdot 4^{n} \\\\\n&= 4^{n}(1 - 3n)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124435 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ into $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Im} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{3} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 3 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 0\\end{array}} \\right.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $[1, 2, 1]^T \\not\\in  \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}),$ we must have that $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the vector $[1, 2, 1]^T$ does not lie in $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$, then $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3.$ Therefore, according to the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3,$ the transformation $\\mathbf T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186124436 :question/problem "$\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection $\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\bbox[3pt,white,border:2px solid Silver]{\\phantom{W W}} = {\\mathbb{R}}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = n \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 0\\end{array}} \\right.$ \n$\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ What should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?"] [17592186124436 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection $\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\bbox[3pt,white,border:2px solid Silver]{ {\\color{blue}\\text{Im} ( \\mathbf{T} )}} = {\\mathbb{R}}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = n \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 0\\end{array}} \\right.$ \n$\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}).$"] [17592186124759 :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n$A = {(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: y > 2x + 1}, ∂A = {(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: y = 2x + 1}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The point $P(0, 1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n2. The point $P(0, 1)$ belongs to $A$\n3. The point $Q(-2, 0)$ is an interior point of $A$"] [17592186124760 :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{3}-y$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨2,2⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2)-f(0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,2⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\ (2(2)^{3}-2)-(2(0)^{3}-1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 15 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨6{x}^{2},-1⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨6{c}_{1}^{2},-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 15 & =⟨6{c}_{1}^{2},-1⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 15 & =12{c}_{1}^{2}-1 \\\\ 16 & =12{c}_{1}^{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}<0<2,$ it's clear that $-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ corresponds to the $x$-coordinate of a point that does not lie on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Therefore, we can ignore it. Thus, ${c}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}$ to find ${c}_{2}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{2-1} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},$ we obtain that ${c}_{2}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}+1=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}+1.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}+1).$"] [17592186124765 :question/problem "Let $D$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}],$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. The identity matrix ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $D.$\n\nII. For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A,B\\in D,$ we have $A+O=O.$\n\nIII. For any $A,B\\in D,$ we have $A+B=B+A.$"] [17592186124765 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, every element $A$ in $D$ satisfies ${a}_{12}={a}_{21}=0$ and ${a}_{11}={a}_{22}.$ So, ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in D.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the identity matrix ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in D.$ Then\n\n$$\n{I}_{2}+O=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\ne O.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, For any matrices $A,B\\in D,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\nA+B=B+A\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186124772 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{6}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{6}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{1}{7}-\\frac{6}{7}=-\\frac{5}{7}.$"] [17592186124773 :question/problem "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0.9 & 0.2 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0.6 & 0.6\\end{bmatrix}, q = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a + b - c$?"] [17592186124782 :question/problem "Solve the recurrence relation\n\n$a_{n} = 6a_{n - 1} - 9a_{n - 2}, a_{0} = 2, a_{1} = 3$."] [17592186124783 :question/problem "Consider the recurrence relation\n\n$a_{n} =-8a_{n - 1} - 16a_{n - 2}, a_{0} = 4, a_{1} = 8$.\n\nGiven that the solution takes the form\n\n$a_{n} = A \\cdot (-4)^{n} + Bn \\cdot (-4)^{n}$,\n\nfind the value of $A + 2B$."] [17592186124790 :question/problem "> $T$ is a bijection $\\hspace{2em} \\Longleftrightarrow  \\hspace{2em} \\{  \\hspace{0.278em} \\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Im} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = n \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = \\bbox[3pt,white,border:2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 0\\end{array}$\n> $⟺$ $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T: R^{n} → R^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?"] [17592186124805 :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n$A = {(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: y^{2} > 4x}, ∂A = {(x, y) ∈ R^{2}: y^{2} = 4x}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The point $P(3, 2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n2. The point $P(3, 2)$ belongs to $A$\n3. The point $Q(-1, 0)$ is an interior point of $A$"] [17592186124828 :question/problem "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the hyperbolic cylinder $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{2}=1?$"] [17592186124849 :question/problem "Solve the recurrence relation\n$a_{n} = a_{n - 1} + 3n^{2} + n - 2, a_{1} =-3, n \\ge 2$."] [17592186124850 :question/problem "Solve the recurrence relation\n$a_{n} = a_{n - 1} - 2, a_{1} = 3, n \\ge 2$."] [17592186124851 :question/problem "Solve the recurrence relation\n$a_{n} = a_{n - 1} + 4n - 3, a_{1} = 2, n \\ge 2$."] [17592186124853 :question/problem "Consider the recurrence relation\n$a_{n} = a_{n - 1} + 5^{n}, a_{1} = 10, n \\ge 2$.\nWhat is the solution to this recurrence relation for $n \\ge 1$?The solution is\n$a_{n} =$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186124854 :question/problem "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 3 - 5 t \\\\ y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} + 4 t \\\\ z = 1 - 2 t\\end{array} \\right.$\nWhat would be the vector equation of the line?"] [17592186124855 :question/problem "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = - 2 + 6 t \\\\ y = 4 t \\\\ z = 3 - t\\end{array} \\right.$\nFind a vector that is parallel to the line."] [17592186124863 :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{5} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0$ is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is not invertible” is true. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(A)=5$ is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is not invertible” only."] [17592186124871 :question/problem "Given $D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 10 & 9 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -4 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,$ what is $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(D))?$"] [17592186124878 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the hyperbolic cylinder $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1?$"] [17592186124881 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}=1?$"] [17592186124882 :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50}=1$ and the plane $x=2$ onto the $yz$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?"] [17592186124883 :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{5}=1$, find its $x$-intercepts."] [17592186124886 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{2}=1$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis."] [17592186124887 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+(z+2)^{2}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$"] [17592186124887 :question/worked-solution "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+(z+2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (z+2)^{2} & =1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ z+2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1$ and its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is \n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\}.\n$$"] [17592186124889 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the ellipsoid $\\frac{x^{2}}{25} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} + ((z + 1)^{2})/(4) = 1$?"] [17592186124891 :question/problem "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z+1=\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}.$"] [17592186124892 :question/problem "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{8}=1?$"] [17592186124893 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the elliptic cone $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=(z-4)^{2}\\,?$"] [17592186124895 :question/problem "Let $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$ and $C = {c_{1}, c_{2}}$ be bases of $R^{2}$.\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{B → C}$ from $B$ to $C$ given that\n\n$[b_{1}]_{C} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, [b_{2}]_{C} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186124900 :question/problem "Consider the set ${p_{1}(t), p_{2}(t)}$ of polynomials from $R[t]$, where\n$p_{1}(t) = 1 - t + t^{2}, p_{2}(t) = 2 - 3t$.\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\frac{x_{1}}{x_{2}}$ from a non-zero solution of $x_{1} \\cdot p_{1}(t) + x_{2} \\cdot p_{2}(t) = 0$."] [17592186124914 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ is given by \n${P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.$\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$  ."] [17592186124923 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z-3=\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}?$"] [17592186124931 :question/problem "Given the elliptic paraboloid $z+4=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4}$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis."] [17592186124956 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that\n$∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,i+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,j$\nand\n$r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j.$"] [17592186124968 :question/problem "$P_{B → C} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$ to the basis $C = {c_{1}, c_{2}, c_{3}}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x = 5b_{1} - b_{2}$."] [17592186124988 :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$"] [17592186125001 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(xyz)$ is defined only when $-1\\le xyz\\le 1.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le xyz\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{yz}{\\sqrt{1-(xyz)^{2}}}$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-(xyz)^{2} & >0 \\\\ (xyz)^{2} & <1 \\\\ |xyz| & <1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives $-1<xyz<1.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domains of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are the same:\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le xyz\\le 1\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186125002 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$"] [17592186125002 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]=-2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{12}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186125003 :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x, y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$. Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if\n$f_{x}(x, y)|= x + ay^{2} - 1,; f_{y}(x, y)|= bxy - 5y + 3,; f_{x}(1, 1)|= 2$."] [17592186125011 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186125012 :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $λ$ of the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186125013 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186125016 :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $λ$ of the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186125017 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186125031 :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$"] [17592186125041 :question/problem "$B = {\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}, C = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}, [x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of $R^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}$."] [17592186125042 :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues $λ_{1} =-4$, $λ_{2} =-2$, and $λ_{3} = 2$.\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D = P^{-1}AP$."] [17592186125044 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which $λ_{1} = 3$, $λ_{2} =-3$, $λ_{3} =-6$ are eigenvalues, and\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a, b, c$ given that\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & a & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & b & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}c & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & a & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & b & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}$?"] [17592186125084 :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6y & 3{y}^{2}-6x\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-6y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-6x.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6y &=0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6x &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-6y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})}^{2}-6x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-2x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}-8x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{3}-8) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}=0,\\,\\,x=2\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(2,2)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-6y)=6x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-6x)=-6 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-6y)=-6\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-6x)=6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6x & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=0\\cdot 0-(-6)^{2}=-36<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(2,2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,2)=[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(2,2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,2)=det({f}^{″}(2,2))=12\\cdot 12-(-6)^{2}=108>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(2,2)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,2)=({f}^{″}(2,2))_{11}=12>0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(2,2).$"] [17592186125086 :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -4 \\\\ 24 & -10\\end{bmatrix}$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$"] [17592186125088 :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$"] [17592186125098 :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$"] [17592186125103 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4x - y \\\\ -8x + 2y\\end{bmatrix}$\nand the vectors\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T$?"] [17592186125106 :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $\\,f(x)={x}^{22}-{x}^{20}+3x-2\\,$, find $f(A).$"] [17592186125110 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $T$ and the vector $v$ given below. If $T$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T$, find $T(v)$.\n\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, v = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186125112 :question/problem "Find the standard matrix that represents the linear transformation $T$, where\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2z + 3y - x \\\\ 2y - 3z + x \\\\ z - x + 3y\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186125118 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{8}$ into ${R}^{8},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{8} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=8 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$ is false. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)<8$ is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 8.$ In turn, this means that $\\text{rank}(T)<8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(T)<8$ only."] [17592186125124 :question/problem "$[T]_{B \\mid C} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}, [x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W$, respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B \\mid C}$ of the linear map $T: V → W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x ∈ V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}$."] [17592186125129 :question/problem "$[T]_{B \\mid C} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}, [x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W$, respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B \\mid C}$ of the linear map $T: V → W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x ∈ V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}$."] [17592186125130 :question/problem "$B = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}, [T]_{B \\mid C} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of $R^{2}$ (shown above) and a basis $C$ of $R^{3}$.\nThe matrix $[T]_{B \\mid C}$ of the linear map $T: R^{2} → R^{3}$ relative to these bases, and the vector $x ∈ R^{2}$, are also given. Find $[T(x)]_{C}$."] [17592186125134 :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-3y - 2z \\\\ x - 2z - 4y \\\\ z - 3x + 4y\\end{bmatrix}$, find $nullity(T)$."] [17592186125136 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x - 2y + 3z \\\\ 4x + y + z \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand the vectors\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhich of the given vectors belong to $Ker(T)$?"] [17592186125138 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x-4y+2z \\\\ x-3y+z \\\\ 4x-4y+z\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$"] [17592186125142 :question/problem "Consider the basis $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of $R^{2}$ given by\n$C = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}$.\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B \\mid C}$ of the map $T: V → R^{2}$ relative to these bases if\n$T(b_{1}) = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, T(b_{2}) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186125144 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{4}$ into ${R}^{4},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=4 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$ is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 4$ is false. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$ is false. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$ only."] [17592186125164 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{2}$ into ${R}^{2},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=2 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$ is false. Since the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ does not lie in $\\text{Im}(T)$, then $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2}.$ Therefore, according to the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 2$ is true. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 2.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2},$ the transformation $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 2$ only."] [17592186125216 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$ \n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or inside the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le 1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/04/0485539c238417b1b93904f9a0e889198c64ff29a4629636094d33048d0bdd32.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0.5,0.5)$ and $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/04/0485539c238417b1b93904f9a0e889198c64ff29a4629636094d33048d0bdd32.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $Q(-1,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})$ satisfy the equation $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(0.5,0.5)$  satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le 1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $A$ and points that do not belong to $A.$ The point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'"] [17592186125217 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{9}+(y+2)^{2}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{9}=1$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis."] [17592186125218 :question/problem "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $z=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}.$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?"] [17592186125231 :question/problem "Let $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$ be a basis of $R^{3}$. The matrix $[T]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T: R^{3} → R^{3}$ is given by\n\n$[T]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind the value of $a + b + c$, given that\n\n$T(b_{1})|= b_{2} - b_{3},; T(b_{2})|= b_{1} - b_{2} - b_{3},; T(b_{3})|= b_{1} + b_{2}$."] [17592186125232 :question/problem "Find the standard matrix that represents the linear transformation $T$, where\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}y - x \\\\ y + x\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186125238 :question/problem "Given the elliptic cone $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}=(z+3)^{2},$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis."] [17592186125719 :tutorial/content "How do we calculate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)$ and $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to-\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)?$ To find out, let's look at the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea736c37f855415e54d8b3a3775fab07159b5de4a368bee45bba13ae261372da.png)\n\nWe see that the curve approaches zero as $x$ approaches positive infinity. The same is true as $x$ approaches negative infinity. So, we write \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)=0 \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe same is true for $\\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ where $n$ is *any* positive integer. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)=0\\, & \\text{and}\\,{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)=0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)=0\\, & \\text{and}\\,{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)=0 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so on. This is because as the size of $x$ increases, the size of $\\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ decreases."] [17592186125728 :tutorial/content "Since the gradient of a function of several variables is made up of partial derivatives, we can expect it to have properties that are similar to partial derivatives.\n\nAssume that $f$ and $g$ are functions whose first-order partial derivatives exist and are continuous on some open domain, and that $a,b,$ and $c$ are real constants. Then, the gradient has the following properties:\n\n- Constant rule:  $\\nabla c = \\mathbf 0$\n\n- Linearity: $\\nabla (af + bg) = a \\nabla\\! f + b\\nabla\\! g$\n\n- Product Rule: $\\nabla(fg) = g \\nabla\\! f + f \\nabla\\! g$\n\n- Quotient Rule: $\\nabla \\left( \\dfrac{f}{g}\\right) = \\dfrac{ g \\nabla\\! f - f \\nabla\\! g}{g^2}$"] [17592186125743 :tutorial/content "To calculate the limit at infinity of a rational function, such as\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{x^4}{x^5-x^2}\\right),\n$$\n\n we apply the following procedure:\n\n**Step 1:** Divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the *denominator*.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}-{x}^{2}}\\right) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}÷{x}^{5}}{({x}^{5}-{x}^{2})÷{x}^{5}}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}}}{\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}}}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{\\frac{1}{x}}{1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Apply the rule that if $n$ is any positive integer then $\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^n}\\right)=0.$ Evaluate the remaining expression to obtain the answer.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}-{x}^{2}}\\right) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{\\frac{1}{x}}{1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186125784 :tutorial/content "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is **invertible** if there is another linear transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}))=\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(\\mathbf{v}))=\\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nfor every vector $\\mathbf{v}\\in \\mathbb R^n.$ The transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is called the **inverse** of $\\mathbf T.$ An invertible linear transformation is also called a **bijection**. \n\nWe have the following theorem regarding invertible linear transformations: \n\n*If $T$ is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf T$, then $\\mathbf T$ is invertible if and only if $T$ is invertible*.\n\nRecall that the invertible matrix theorem in terms of dimension, rank, and nullity states that if $T$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, then \n\n$T$ is invertible $\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$\n$$\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Col} ( T ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{dim(Col} ( T ) ) = n = \\text{rank} ( T ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Null} ( T ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{dim(Null} ( T ) ) = 0 = \\text{nullity} ( T ) .\\end{array}} \\right.\n$$\n\nIn the theorem above, we can make the following swaps, where $\\mathbf T$ is a linear transformation with the standard matrix $T{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\text{Col} ( T ) & \\displaystyle  = \\text{Im} ( \\mathbf{T} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( T ) & \\displaystyle  = \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\text{Null} ( T ) & \\displaystyle  = \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( T ) & \\displaystyle  = \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} )\\end{array}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the **invertible matrix theorem in terms of linear transformations:**\n\n $\\mathbf T$ is a bijection (invertible) $\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Im} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = n \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 0\\end{array}} \\right.$ \n$\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$  $T$ is invertible"] [17592186125797 :tutorial/content "What is the matrix of the linear transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} \\longrightarrow {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 x + 4 y \\\\ - x - y\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nrelative to the basis\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{ {\\color{red}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}, {\\color{blue}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}} \\right\\}\\:?\n$$\n\nIn other words, what is the matrix $[T]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ such that \n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{B}} = [T]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}?\n$$\n\nIn fact, the answer is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the images of the basis vectors $\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2$ under the transformation relative to the basis:\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}| & | \\\\ {\\color{red}{[ T ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{1} ) ]}_{\\mathcal{B}}} & {\\color{blue}{[ T ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{2} ) ]}_{\\mathcal{B}}} \\\\ | & |\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThis matrix is called the **matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$** or the **$\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}.$**\n\nAs a result, for the transformation $\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]} \\longrightarrow {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 x + 4 y \\\\ - x - y\\end{array} \\right]}$, with respect to the basis \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{ {\\color{red} \\mathbf{b}_1} = {\\color{red}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}, {\\color{blue} \\mathbf{b}_2} = {\\color{blue}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}} \\right\\},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n$$\n{\\color{red} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_1 \\right)} =\t{\\color{red} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}7 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]} }\\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad {\\color{blue} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right) } = {\\color{blue} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}11 \\\\ - 3\\end{array} \\right]} }.\n$$\n\nNow, to write ${\\color{red} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_1 \\right)}$ and ${\\color{blue} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right) }$ with respect to our basis $\\mathcal{B},$ we assume that \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\left[\\mathbf{T}\\left( \\mathbf{b}_1 \\right)\\right]_{\\mathcal{B}}} = {\\color{red}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\alpha }_{1} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}} \n\\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \n{\\color{blue}\\left[\\mathbf{T}\\left( \\mathbf{b}_2\\right)\\right]_{\\mathcal{B}}} = {\\color{blue}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}},\n$$\nwhere the components $\\alpha_1,$ $\\alpha_2,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ satisfy\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\alpha_1} \\mathbf{b}_1+{\\color{red}\\alpha_2}\\mathbf{b}_2 = {\\color{red} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_1 \\right)}\\quad \\text{and}\\quad {\\color{blue}\\beta_1}\\mathbf{b}_1+{\\color{blue}\\beta_2} \\mathbf{b}_2 = {\\color{blue} \\mathbf{T} \\left( \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSolving these two systems gives\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\left[\\mathbf{T}\\left( \\mathbf{b}_1 \\right)\\right]_{\\mathcal{B}}}= {\\color{red}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\alpha }_{1} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}} = {\\color{red}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}16 \\\\ - 9\\end{array} \\right]}} \n\\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \n{\\color{blue}\\left[\\mathbf{T}\\left( \\mathbf{b}_2\\right)\\right]_{\\mathcal{B}}} ={\\color{blue}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}}= {\\color{blue}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}25 \\\\ - 14\\end{array} \\right]}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{red}16} & {\\color{blue}25} \\\\ {\\color{red}- 9} & {\\color{blue}- 14}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$"] [17592186125798 :tutorial/content "In general, consider a basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\dots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}$ of a vector space $V$ and a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:V \\rightarrow V$. For any $\\mathbf{x}\\in V$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = x_1\\mathbf{b}_1+x_2\\mathbf{b}_2+\\cdots + x_n\\mathbf{b}_n\n$$\n\nor, equivalently $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{array} \\right]}$.\nThen the matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ is given by\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} =\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}| & | &  & | \\\\ [ \\mathbf{T} ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{1} ) {]}_{\\mathcal{B}} & [ \\mathbf{T} ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{2} ) {]}_{\\mathcal{B}} & \\cdots  & [ \\mathbf{T} ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{n} ) {]}_{\\mathcal{B}} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{B}} = \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}| & | &  & | \\\\ [ \\mathbf{T} ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{1} ) {]}_{\\mathcal{B}} & [ \\mathbf{T} ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{2} ) {]}_{\\mathcal{B}} & \\cdots  & [ \\mathbf{T} ( {\\mathbf{b}}_{n} ) {]}_{\\mathcal{B}} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\large[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}}\n\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{array} \\right]}}_{[\\large\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}}.\n$$"] [17592186125815 :tutorial/content "Suppose we know that the gradient of a function $f(x,y)$ is \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = (2xy-3x^2) \\ \\mathbf i + (x^2-2y) \\ \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\n \n\nHow can we reconstruct the function $f(x,y)$ knowing only its gradient?\n\nSince the components of $\\nabla f$ must be the first-order partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y$ respectively, we know that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x} =  2xy-3x^2,   \\qquad \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}=x^2-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating  $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ as a constant, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\int \\left(2xy-3x^2\\right) \\textrm d  x = x^2y -x^3 +g(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(y)$ is an arbitrary function of $y.$ Intuitively, $g(y)$ contains all the terms that would vanish when differentiated with respect to $x.$\n\nNow, differentiating the above with respect to $y$ gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} = x^2 + g'(y).\n$$\n\nSo, equating the two expressions for $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \nx^2 + g'(y) &= x^2-2y\\\\\n        g'(y)  & = -2y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\displaystyle g(y)=\\int(-2y) \\ \\textrm d y =  -y^2 +C.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^2y -x^3-y^2 +C .\n$$\n\nNote that the above procedure is symmetric with respect to $x$ and $y.$ We could have started by integrating $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y$ and then differentiating with respect to $x.$ The final result would have been the same."] [17592186125888 :tutorial/content "The most common quadric surfaces are those centered at the origin. We describe these common surfaces in the table below.\n\n| Surface | Equation | Image |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Ellipsoid | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}+\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} = 1$ | ![](images/09/09dc100248a28a5518bcd8428368edefc7c3ee8d5ef0e50515cb7552ee54a376.png) |\n| Elliptic Paraboloid | $z = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](images/76/76e4ad8cb1e83eadae4dce865e100c9a716f9830e7f12adb367b2a9b74d1a651.png) |\n| Hyperbolic Paraboloid | $z = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}-\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](images/cb/cbc04d7f98e1bb7ce0d62eda71102a6abad708dfcedd7aabee8fff23281471dd.png) |\n| Hyperboloid of One Sheet | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}-\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1$ | ![](images/df/df6ef914bc78e121d75baf9f5147784d0e6e434d76800eff60dcb3c3d764477c.png) |\n| Hyperboloid of Two Sheets | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}-\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=-1$ | ![](images/08/0870b95c5ce1133d6645e3063174157e36c4354df370c05e48218111ea714838.png) |\n| Elliptic Cone | $\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](images/0f/0f492bf07e573ec7858977e48fa4bf81635e61520b5caa9edef14bd259184347.png) |\n\nAlso, recall the following from previous lessons:\n\n- We can generate paraboloids, hyperboloids, and cones with alternative orientation axes by permuting the variables in the corresponding equation. \n\nFor example, the equation of an elliptic cone with its vertex at $O$ and axis of symmetry along the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}.\n$$\n\n- We can shift the center/vertex/saddle point of a quadric surface to $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ using the usual coordinate transformation:\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) \\longrightarrow (x-x_0,y-y_0,z-z_0)\n$$\n\nFinally, there is a simple way to distinguish between hyperboloids with one and two sheets by simply looking at the equations:\n\n- *one* minus sign indicates hyperboloids of *one* sheet:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1\n$$\n\n- *two* minus signs indicate hyperboloids of *two* sheets:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-}1\n$$"] [17592186125891 :tutorial/content "The **second partial derivatives test** helps to determine whether a stationary point of a function of two variables gives rise to a local maximum, a local minimum, or a saddle point.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a}$ is a critical point of $f(\\mathbf x),$ and let $H(\\mathbf{a})$ denote the determinant of $\\boldsymbol f''(x,y)$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{a}$ (recall that $H(\\mathbf a)$ is called the *Hessian determinant* of $f$ at $\\mathbf a$).\n\nThe second partial derivative test states the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$  then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a},$ and\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$  then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive, and we need to classify the point using some other method.\n\nAs an example, consider the function $f (x, y) = 3 + 2x^2 - x y + y^2.$ Let's find the critical points of this function and then classify them using the second partial derivatives test.\n\nWe start by computing the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 4x -y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = 2y -x\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-y &=0 \\\\ 2y-x &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $x=0, y=0.$ Hence, $(0,0)$ is the only stationary point.  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-y)=4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(0,0)) = 4 \\cdot 2 - (-1)^2 = 7 > 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(0,0) > 0$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(0,0) = (\\boldsymbol f''(0,0))_{11}= 4 >0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$"] [17592186128838 :question/problem "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 v \\cos   u \\\\ v \\sin   u\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where ${\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} =  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$"] [17592186128838 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]} \\to {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( u , v ) \\\\ y ( u , v )\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = 2v\\cos{u}$ and $y(u,v) = v\\sin{u}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡u-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\  & =-2v({sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u) \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when ${\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}u \\\\ v\\end{array} \\right]}  = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\\right|_{(0,-1)} = -2(-1) = 2.\n$$"] [17592186128858 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$\\displaystyle\n\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{y}^{1} \\int_{0}^{y} 3e^{x^3} \\: \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y.$\n\n*Hint: Change change the order of integration to $\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x.$*"] [17592186128867 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass, denoted $\\overline{x},$ of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_O = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =2, & \\,{m}_{2} & =4, & \\,{m}_{3} & =3, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-1, & \\,{x}_{2} & =2, & \\,{x}_{3} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =2+4+3 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the total moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2+8+12 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m} = \\dfrac{18}{9}=2.\n$$"] [17592186128868 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $(x_1y_1),(x_2,y_2),\\ldots,(x_n,y_n)$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left(\\dfrac{M_y}{m},\\dfrac{M_x}{m}\\right),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_x = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i y_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_y = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\nm_1 = 3, \\qquad m_2 = 5, \\qquad m_3 = 1.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $y$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\ny_1 = 3, \\qquad y_2 = 0, \\qquad y_3 = 3.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =3+5+1 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{y}_{1}+{m}_{2}{y}_{2}+{m}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3+5\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =9+0+3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{y} = \\dfrac{M_x}{m} = \\dfrac{12}{9} = \\dfrac{4}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186128871 :question/problem "What is the value of  $\\dfrac{4}{3} \\times (-5+3)^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3} ?$"] [17592186128871 :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3} \\times ({\\color{red}-5} + {\\color{red}3})^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{4}{3} \\times ({\\color{red}-2})^{-2} + \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that $(-2)^{-2}=\\dfrac{1}{(-2)^2} = \\dfrac{1}{4}.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}\\times (-2)^{-2}+\\frac{2}{3} &= \\frac{4}{3}\\times \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{4\\times 1}{3\\times 4}+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{\\cancel{4}\\times 1}{3\\times \\cancel{4}}+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ &= 1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186128892 :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=6{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\n- $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+{t}^{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{2}(t)+1\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\n- $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$"] [17592186128898 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-3)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x) \\neq f(3)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-3$ is a removable discontinuity. A similar argument applies for $x=3$, which is also a removable discontinuity. So, the function has removable discontinuities at $x=-3, 3.$ \n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but it is not a removable discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(0)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=0$ is not a removable discontinuity."] [17592186128899 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)= 1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x) \\neq f(2)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a point discontinuity."] [17592186128900 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x),$ defined by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3, & x<0 \\\\ 0, & x=0 \\\\ 3-x, & x>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^+} f(x)=\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^-} f(x)$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)=f(0)$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0$"] [17592186128900 :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(0)=0$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(x+3)=3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(3-x)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3. \\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is $\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3,$ while the value of the function is $f(0)=0.$ So, statement II is false and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)\\neq f(0)\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\nSince the third condition failed, the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=0,$ and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."] [17592186128928 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2} \\sqrt{x-2}$."] [17592186128929 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} \\sqrt{3-x}$."] [17592186128932 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -3} \\sqrt[3]{3x-2x^2}$."] [17592186128975 :question/problem "Find $B=\\overline{A},$ where $A={\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}4 \\text{i} & 3 - \\text{i} & 1 + \\text{i} \\\\ 2 - 2 \\text{i} & 2 - \\text{i} & 1 + 2 \\text{i} \\\\ \\text{i} & 3 \\text{i} & 2 \\text{i}\\end{array} \\right]}.$ What is the value of $b_{11} + b_{23}?$"] [17592186128983 :question/problem "Evaluate the repeated integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{4}  \\int_{0}^{2} \\dfrac{x^3}{y^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x  \\, \\textrm{d}y.$"] [17592186128984 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} \\dfrac y{x^2} \\, \\textrm d A$, where \n$D=\\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\:2 \\leq x \\leq 4,\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 3\\right\\}.$"] [17592186128985 :question/problem "Which of the following equalities are true given that\n$D = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}.$\n\nI. $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} e^{\\sqrt{xy}} \\,\\text{d}A  = \\int_{0}^{1}  \\int_{0}^{2}e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,  \\text{d}y \\, \\text{d}x$\n\nII. $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,\\text{d}A  = \\int_{0}^{2}  \\int_{0}^{1} e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\, \\text{d}y\\,  \\text{d}x$\n\nIII. $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} e^{\\sqrt{xy}} \\,\\text{d}A = \\int_{0}^{1}  \\int_{0}^{2}e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,  \\text{d}x \\, \\text{d}y$"] [17592186128989 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{R} xy\\ \\!e^{x+y}\\ \\textrm d A$ over the rectangle $R= [0,1] \\times [1,2].$"] [17592186128990 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{R} \\left(x^2-y^2 +3\\right)  \\textrm d A,$  where $R= [-1,3] \\times [2, 4].$"] [17592186128991 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{R}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A$, where\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =  {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}2 , \\hspace{1em} & \\text{ if} \\hspace{1em} 0 < x < 2 , \\hspace{0.167em} \\hspace{0.167em} - 1 < y < 0 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{x y + x + y + 1}} , \\hspace{1em} & \\text{ if} \\hspace{1em} 0 < x < 2 , \\hspace{0.167em} \\hspace{0.167em} \\hspace{0.167em} 0 < y < 1 ,\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$"] [17592186128992 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\sum_{j=1}^3\\sum_{i=1}^2 (i+j).$"] [17592186128997 :question/worked-solution "The tangent plane to a surface is horizontal if and only if the normal vector to the tangent plane is vertical. So, let's first find the general expression of a normal vector to the surface.\n\nRemember that the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0), \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0), -1 \\right\\rangle$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =2y+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n\\left\\langle y, \\: 2y+x, \\: -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe want to find where this normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  x}} = 0 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  f}{\\partial  y}} = 0\\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 0 \\\\ 2 y + x = 0\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$ \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2(0) +x = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz = 0^2+(0)(0)= 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$"] [17592186129000 :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where $r \\geq 0$ and $0 \\leq \\theta <         2\\pi.$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](images/d6/d60214a4b32c4462d422372aa90e78944649811ce0b1e277f07fe90663e5be94.png)\n\n  \n\n- The ray from the origin through $N'$ makes an angle of $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to $N'$ is $r=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=3$ because $N$ lies $3$ units *above* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $N$ are $\\left(\\sqrt{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, 3\\right).$"] [17592186129003 :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where $\\rho \\geq 0,$ $0 \\leq \\theta <   2\\pi,$ and $0 \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi.$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following: \n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $P$ equals $4$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $P$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}2-\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{10}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $P$ forms an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(4, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{10}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\right).$"] [17592186129008 :question/problem "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_0^{1} \\int_{\\sqrt{x}}^{1 } 9\\sqrt{y^3+1}\\,\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x}.$"] [17592186129009 :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1\\int_{2x}^{3-x} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x .$"] [17592186129010 :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n$\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\int_{y^3}^{1} f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y.$"] [17592186129011 :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d180fd5de88ebc465df522879142e5a4c5446c758aaccde792092968078c254.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed by the curves $x=2y^3-2y$ and  $x=y^3-4y,$ and the $y$-axis, as shown in the picture. Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm{d}A.$"] [17592186129014 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\iiint\\limits_{R} y \\cos{x} \\sin{z} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the box $R = \\bigg\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq \\pi \\bigg\\}.$"] [17592186129016 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/8} \\int_0^{\\sin2\\theta} r \\, \\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta.$"] [17592186129017 :question/problem "![](images/17/1782385c55493892be0308ede1e4fbe5308ea9440e21b70cc2da7d7f01fb8dcd.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D y \\sqrt{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?"] [17592186129023 :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\sqrt[3]{x-1}$."] [17592186129039 :question/problem "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+2{y}_{j-1})\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},\\, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i}+2{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute $\\displaystyle\\iint\\limits_R f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d A.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n\\left(x_{i-1} + x_i\\right)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j = (x_m^2 - x_0^2)(y_n-y_0)\n$$"] [17592186129042 :question/problem "Solve the recurrence relation\n$a_n = a_{n-2}, \\qquad a_0=2, \\quad a_1=4$."] [17592186129046 :question/problem "Consider the recurrence relation\n$a_n = -6a_{n-1} - 12a_{n-2}, \\quad a_0 =-1, \\quad a_1=3+\\sqrt{3}$.\nGiven that the solution takes the form\n$a_n = \\big(2\\sqrt{3}\\big)^n \\left(A\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5n\\pi}{6}\\right) + B\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5n\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right)$,\nfind the value of $A + B$."] [17592186129046 :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the recurrence relation is\n\n$$\na_n = \\big(2\\sqrt{3}\\big)^n \\left(A\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5n\\pi}{6}\\right) + B\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5n\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right)\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $a_0 = -1$ into the general solution gives\n$(2\\sqrt{3})^{0}(A\\cos \\left(\\frac{0 \\cdot 5π}{6}\\right) + B\\sin \\left(\\frac{0 \\cdot 5π}{6}\\right))|=-1; 1 \\cdot (A \\cdot 1 + B \\cdot 0)|=-1; A|=-1$.\n- Substituting $a_1=3+\\sqrt{3}$ and $A=-1$ into the general solution gives\n$(2\\sqrt{3})^{1}(-1 \\cdot \\cos \\left(\\frac{1 \\cdot 5π}{6}\\right) + B\\sin \\left(\\frac{1 \\cdot 5π}{6}\\right))|= 3 + \\sqrt{3}; 2\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + B \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})|= 3 + \\sqrt{3}; 3 + \\sqrt{3}B|= 3 + \\sqrt{3}; B|= 1$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA + B = -1 + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= (2\\sqrt{3})^{n}(\\sin \\left(\\frac{5nπ}{6}\\right) - \\cos \\left(\\frac{5nπ}{6}\\right))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129047 :question/problem "Solve the recurrence relation\n$a_n =2a_{n-1} - 2a_{n-2}, \\quad a_0=2, \\quad a_1=5$."] [17592186129047 :question/worked-solution "Moving all terms of our recurrence relation to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\na_n - 2a_{n-1} + 2a_{n-2} = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 2\\lambda + 2 = 0\n$$\n\nThis equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 2λ + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} - 2λ + 1) - 1 + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)^{2} + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)^{2} &= -1 \\\\\nλ - 1 &= ± \\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= 1 ± \\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 1 + \\textrm{i}, \\qquad \\lambda_2 = 1 - \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nExpressing our solutions in polar form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ_{1} &= \\sqrt{2}(\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) + \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)), \\\\\nλ_{2} &= \\sqrt{2}(\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) - \\textrm{i}\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to the recurrence relation is\n\n$$\na_n = \\big(\\sqrt{2}\\big)^n \\left(A\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{4}\\right) + B\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\nWe now find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions:\n\n- Substituting $a_0 = 2$ into the general solution gives\n$(\\sqrt{2})^{0}(A\\cos \\left(\\frac{0 \\cdot π}{4}\\right) + B\\sin \\left(\\frac{0 \\cdot π}{4}\\right))|= 2; 1 \\cdot (A \\cdot 1 + B \\cdot 0)|= 2; A|= 2$.\n- Substituting $a_1=5$ and $A=2$ into the general solution gives\n$(\\sqrt{2})^{1}(2 \\cdot \\cos \\left(\\frac{1 \\cdot π}{4}\\right) + B\\sin \\left(\\frac{1 \\cdot π}{4}\\right))|= 5; \\sqrt{2} \\cdot (\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2} + B \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})|= 5; 2 + B|= 5; B|= 3$.\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\na_n = \\big(\\sqrt{2}\\big)^n \\left(2\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{4}\\right) + 3\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{4}\\right)\\right)\n$$"] [17592186129050 :question/problem "Solve the difference equation\n$a_n + 4a_{n-1} + 4a_{n-2} = 18, \\quad a_0 = 1, \\quad a_1 = -2$."] [17592186129050 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the general solution $a_n^{(h)}$ of the associated homogeneous difference equation\n\n$$\na_n + 4a_{n-1} + 4a_{n-2} = 0\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 4λ + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 2)^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -2\n$$\n\nis a repeated root, and therefore\n\n$$\na_n^{(h)} = A \\cdot (-2)^n + B n \\cdot (-2)^n\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $a_n^{(p)}$ of the original difference equation.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0$. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0$, i.e.\n\n$$\na_n^{(p)} = \\alpha\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined. Since $a_n^{(p)}$ does not depend on $n$, we also have\n\n$$\na_{n-1}^{(p)} = a_{n-2}^{(p)} = \\alpha\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha$, we substitute the particular solution into the difference equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha + 4\\alpha + 4\\alpha = 18 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\alpha = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na_n^{(p)} = 2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{n}^{(h)} + a_{n}^{(p)} \\\\\n&= A \\cdot (-2)^{n} + Bn \\cdot (-2)^{n} + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial values.\n\n- Substituting $a_0 = 1$ into the general solution gives\n$A \\cdot (-2)^{0} + B \\cdot 0 \\cdot (-2)^{0} + 2|= 1; A + 2|= 1; A|=-1$.\n- Substituting $a_1 = -2$ and $A = -1$ into the general solution gives\n$(-1) \\cdot (-2)^{1} + B \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-2)^{1} + 2|=-2; 2 - 2B + 2|=-2; - 2B|=-6; B|= 3$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the difference equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= (-1) \\cdot (-2)^{n} + 3n \\cdot (-2)^{n} + 2 \\\\\n&= (-2)^{n}(3n - 1) + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129053 :question/problem "Solve the difference equation\n\n$a_n - 16 a_{n-2} = 9\\cdot 5^n, \\quad a_0 = 15, \\quad a_1 = 85$."] [17592186129053 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the general solution $a_n^{(h)}$ of the associated homogeneous difference equation\n\n$$\na_n - 16a_{n-2} = 0\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 16 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 4)(λ - 4) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -4\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na_n^{(h)} = A \\cdot (-4)^n + B \\cdot 4^n\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $a_n^{(p)}$ of the original difference equation.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous difference equation is $9\\cdot 5^n$. The base of the exponential part is $5$, which is not a root of the characteristic equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\na_n^{(p)} = \\alpha \\cdot 5^n\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant that is to be determined. As a result,\n\n$$\na_{n-1}^{(p)} = \\alpha \\cdot 5^{n-1}, \\qquad a_{n-2}^{(p)} = \\alpha \\cdot 5^{n-2}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha$, we substitute the particular solution into the difference equation and solve for $\\alpha{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\alpha  \\cdot  {5}^{n} - 16 \\cdot  \\alpha  \\cdot  {5}^{n - 2} & \\displaystyle  = 9 \\cdot  {5}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\alpha  \\cdot  {5}^{n} - 16 \\alpha  \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{ {5}^{2}}} \\cdot  {5}^{n} & \\displaystyle  = 9 \\cdot  {5}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\alpha  \\cdot  \\cancel{ {5}^{n}} - 16 \\alpha  \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{ {5}^{2}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{ {5}^{n}} & \\displaystyle  = 9 \\cdot  \\cancel{ {5}^{n}} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\alpha  - {\\displaystyle \\frac{16 \\alpha }{25}} & \\displaystyle  = 9 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{9 \\alpha }{25}} & \\displaystyle  = 9 \\\\ \\displaystyle \\alpha  & \\displaystyle  = 25\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\na_n^{(p)} = 25 \\cdot 5^n = 5^{n+2}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{n}^{(h)} + a_{n}^{(p)} \\\\\n&= A \\cdot (-4)^{n} + B \\cdot 4^{n} + 5^{n + 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial values.\n\n- Substituting $a_0 = 15$ into the general solution gives\n$15|= A \\cdot (-4)^{0} + B \\cdot 4^{0} + 5^{0 + 2}; 15|= A + B + 25; A + B|=-10$.\n- Substituting $a_1 = 85$ into the general solution gives\n$85|= A \\cdot (-4)^{1} + B \\cdot 4^{1} + 5^{1 + 2}; 85|=-4A + 4B + 125; - 4A + 4B|=-40; 4A - 4B|= 40; A - B|= 10$.\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}A + B = - 10 \\\\ A - B = 10\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=0$, $B=-10$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the recurrence relation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= -10 \\cdot 4^{n} + 5^{n + 2} \\\\\n&= 5^{n + 2} - 10 \\cdot 4^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129060 :question/worked-solution "We have the Pythagorean triple\n\n$$\n(a,b,c) = (8,15,17)\n$$\n\nComparing our polynomial identity with\n\n$$\na^2+b^2 = c^2\n$$\n\nwe have two possible options:\n\n- Option 1: $\\quad x^2-y^2 = 8, \\qquad 2xy = 15, \\qquad x^2+y^2 = 17$\n- Option 2: $\\quad x^2-y^2 = 15, \\qquad 2xy = 8, \\qquad x^2+y^2 = 17$\n\nWe'll try both options to see which generates the required triple:\n\n- Considering the first option: Adding the first and third equations, we get\n$\\begin{array}{rl} x^2-\\cancel{y^2}&=8 \\\\ x^2+\\cancel{y^2}&=17 \\\\[5pt] \\hline (x^2+x^2) &= (8+17) \\end{array}$\nwhich gives\n$2x^2 = 25\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x^2 = \\dfrac{25}2$,\nand we can disregard this option since $\\dfrac{25}2$ is not a perfect square:\n- Considering the second option: Adding the first and third equations, we get\n$\\begin{array}{rl} x^2-\\cancel{y^2}&=15 \\\\ x^2+\\cancel{y^2}&=17 \\\\[5pt] \\hline (x^2+x^2) &= (15+17) \\end{array}$\nwhich gives\n$2x^2 = 32\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=4$.\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n$2\\cdot 4y = 8 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=1$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $x=p=4$ and $y=q=1$ generate the required triple. Let's just test to make sure:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{2}–y^{2})^{2} + (2xy)^{2} &= (x^{2} + y^{2})^{2} \\\\\n(4^{2}–1^{2})^{2} + (2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 1)^{2} &= (4^{2} + 1^{2})^{2} \\\\\n(16–1)^{2} + 8^{2} &= (16 + 1)^{2} \\\\\n15^{2} + 8^{2} &= 17^{2} \\\\\n8^{2} + 15^{2} &= 17^{2}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\np+q = 4+1 = 5\n$$"] [17592186129401 :question/problem "What is $18 \\div \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{-2} + (\\frac{1}{101} - 0.01)^{0}$?"] [17592186129405 :question/problem "From top to bottom, what are the missing expressions in the following polynomial division problem?\n\n$\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222e