} \\hspace{0.222em} \\phantom{10 {x}^{3} + 79 {x}^{2} + 1} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 28 x - \\phantom{0} 15 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\phantom{10 {x}^{3} + 79 {x}^{2} + 1} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\underset{―}{28 x + 168} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\phantom{10 {x}^{3} + 79 {x}^{2} + 142 x} - 183\\end{array}$"] [17592186129406 :question/problem "Consider the following polynomial division:\n$\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{x + 2} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} 5 x \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle x + 2 \\hspace{0.222em} \\enclose{longdiv}{\\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.167em} 5 {x}^{2} + 10 x + 8 \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{x + 2} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\underset{―}{5 {x}^{2} + 10 x \\phantom{+ 08}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{x + 2} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\phantom{5 {x}^{2} + 10 x +} 8\\end{array}$\n\nFrom the above, $\\frac{5x^{2} + 10x + 8}{x + 2} =$"] [17592186129409 :question/problem "From top to bottom, what are the missing expressions in the following polynomial division problem?\n\n$\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{x + 1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\boxed{\\phantom{4 {x}^{2} - x + 1}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle x + 1 \\hspace{0.222em} \\enclose{longdiv}{\\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.167em} 4 {x}^{3} + 3 {x}^{2} + 0 x - 1 \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{x + 1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\underset{―}{4 {x}^{3} + 4 {x}^{2}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{x + 1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\phantom{4 {x}^{3}} \\phantom{0} - {x}^{2} + 0 x - 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{x + 1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\phantom{4 {x}^{3}} \\underset{―}{\\phantom{0} - {x}^{2} - \\phantom{0} x} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{x + 1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\phantom{4 {x}^{3} + 3 {x}^{2} + 0} \\boxed{\\phantom{x - 1}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{x + 1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\phantom{4 {x}^{3} + 3 {x}^{2} + 0} \\underset{―}{x + 1} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{x + 1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\phantom{4 {x}^{3} + 3 {x}^{2} + 0 x} - \\hspace{0.222em} 2\\end{array}$"] [17592186129426 :question/problem "Consider the recurrence relation\n$a_{n} =-7a_{n - 1} - 12a_{n - 2}, a_{0} =-3, a_{1} = 2$.\nGiven that the solution takes the form\n$a_{n} = A \\cdot (-3)^{n} + B \\cdot (-4)^{n}$,\nfind the value of $A - B$."] [17592186129427 :question/problem "Solve the recurrence relation\n$a_{n} =-4a_{n - 1} + 5a_{n - 2}, a_{0} = 1, a_{1} = 7$."] [17592186129431 :question/problem "Consider the recurrence relation\n$a_{n} = 6a_{n - 1} - 18a_{n - 2}, a_{0} = 1, a_{1} =-3$.\nGiven that the solution takes the form\n$a_{n} = (3\\sqrt{2})^{n}(A\\cos \\left(\\frac{nπ}{4}\\right) + B\\sin \\left(\\frac{nπ}{4}\\right))$,\nfind the value of $A + B$."] [17592186129432 :question/problem "Solve the recurrence relation\n$a_{n} =-25a_{n - 2}, a_{0} =-2, a_{1} = 5$."] [17592186129433 :question/problem "Solve the recurrence relation\n$a_{n} = a_{n - 1} - a_{n - 2}, a_{0} = 0, a_{1} = 2\\sqrt{3}$."] [17592186129442 :question/problem "Given that the difference equation\n\n$a_{n} + 2a_{n - 1} + a_{n - 2} = 8 \\cdot (-1)^{n}$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$a_{n} = A \\cdot (-1)^{n} + Bn \\cdot (-1)^{n} + 4n^{2} \\cdot (-1)^{n}$,\n\nsolve the relation\n$a_{n} + 2a_{n - 1} + a_{n - 2} = 8 \\cdot (-1)^{n}, a_{0} = 1, a_{1} =-7$."] [17592186129443 :question/problem "Solve the difference equation\n\n$a_{n} - 2a_{n - 1} - 8a_{n - 2} = 5 \\cdot 3^{n}, a_{0} =-9, a_{1} = 3$."] [17592186129444 :question/problem "Solve the difference equation\n\n$a_{n} - 5a_{n - 1} + 6a_{n - 2} =-4, a_{0} = 1, a_{1} = 1$."] [17592186129445 :question/problem "Solve the difference equation\n\n$a_{n} - 2a_{n - 1} - 8a_{n - 2} = 9n + 9, a_{0} = 1, a_{1} = 0$."] [17592186129449 :question/problem "Consider the recurrence relation\n$a_{n} = a_{n - 1} - a_{n - 2}, a_{0} = 10, a_{1} = 8$.\nGiven that the solution takes the form\n$a_{n} = A\\cos \\left(\\frac{nπ}{3}\\right) + B\\sin \\left(\\frac{nπ}{3}\\right)$,\nfind the value of $A \\cdot B$."] [17592186129455 :question/problem "Consider the following derivation:\n\n$(x + 3)^{2} - 6x|=; Step 1:\\begin{vmatrix}(x^{2} + 2 \\cdot x \\cdot 3 + 9) - 6x|= \\\\ Step 2:\\end{vmatrix}(x^{2} + 6x + 9) - 6x|=; Step 3:\\begin{vmatrix}x^{2} + (6x - 6x) + 9|= \\\\ Step 4:\\end{vmatrix}x^{2} + 9$\n\nWhich of the following statements is true?"] [17592186129456 :question/problem "Consider the following derivation:\n\n$5x - (x - 5)^{2}|=; Step 1:\\begin{vmatrix}5x - (x^{2} - 2 \\cdot x \\cdot 5 + 25)|= \\\\ Step 2:\\end{vmatrix}5x - (x^{2} + 5x + 25)|=; Step 3:\\begin{vmatrix}-x^{2} + (5x - 5x) - 25|= \\\\ Step 4:\\end{vmatrix}- x^{2} - 25$\n\nWhich of the following statements is true?"] [17592186129466 :question/problem "Consider the vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2 \\cdot 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}5\\textrm{i} \\\\ -7 + 3\\textrm{i} \\\\ 4 - \\textrm{i} \\\\ -6 - 4\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 - 2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 2 - 6\\textrm{i} \\\\ 4 - 6\\textrm{i} \\\\ -5\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $λ_{1} = 2 - 2\\textrm{i}$ and $λ_{3} =-3 + 5\\textrm{i}$, respectively.\nGiven that $A = PBP^{-1}$, which of the following could be the matrix $P$?"] [17592186129489 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = \\sin^{3} t, y = \\cos^{3} t, t ∈ [0, \\frac{π}{2}]$.\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis."] [17592186129505 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$y = \\frac{1}{x}, y = \\frac{2}{x}, y = x, y = 2x$"] [17592186129506 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$u = \\frac{y}{x^{2}}, v = xy$,\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n$y = x^{2}, y = 2x^{2}, y = \\frac{1}{x}, y = \\frac{4}{x}$."] [17592186129511 :question/problem "By integrating first with respect to $z$, then $y$, and then $x$, evaluate the triple integral\n$∭_(R)6x^{2} + 12y\\sqrt{z}dV$\nover the rectangular box $R = {(x, y, z): 1 \\le x \\le 3, 0 \\le y \\le 2, 0 \\le z \\le 1}$."] [17592186129512 :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2fe61de100b4a469e4d79dc67869dd68936a0bf396757c4d08fb4d6cade6bda.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is $∬_(D)xy^{2}dA$ expressed in polar coordinates?"] [17592186129513 :question/problem "If $D = {(x, y): (x - 3)^{2} + y^{2} \\le 9}$, then $∬_(D)(x^{2} - y^{2})dA =$"] [17592186129514 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n$∭_(R)yz^{2}\\cos xdV$,\nover the box $R = {(x, y, z): \\frac{π}{2} \\le x \\le \\frac{3π}{2}, 1 \\le y \\le 5, 0 \\le z \\le 3}$."] [17592186129517 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}\\le 9,\\,0\\le z\\le 2+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}xy\\,\\text{d}V=$"] [17592186129518 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n$∭_(R)\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}dV$\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^{2} + y^{2} = 4$ and between the surfaces $z = 0$ and $z = 12 - 4y$."] [17592186129519 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,1\\le z\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V=$"] [17592186129521 :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following repeated integrals so that they are integrated first with respect to $y$, then $x$, and then $z$, express the following sum as a single repeated integral.\n\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x^{2}}\\displaystyle \\int_{-z}^{x/3}f(x, y, z)dydzdx + \\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2 - x}\\displaystyle \\int_{-z}^{x/3}f(x, y, z)dydzdx$"] [17592186129522 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x^{2}/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{\\sqrt{5x + 9}}^{3\\sqrt{x + 1}}ye^{4z - z^{2}}dydzdx$.\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $dydxdz$.*"] [17592186129529 :question/problem "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{z}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*"] [17592186129541 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =2, & \\,{m}_{2} & =5, & \\,{m}_{3} & =7, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1.5, & \\,{x}_{2} & =5, & \\,{x}_{3} & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =2+5+7 \\\\  & =14\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1.5+5\\cdot 5+7\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =3+25+70 \\\\  & =98\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{98}{14}=7.\n$$"] [17592186129542 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =2, & \\,{m}_{2} & =3, & \\,{m}_{3} & =1, & \\,{m}_{4} & =5, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1, & \\,{x}_{2} & =3, & \\,{x}_{3} & =5, & \\,{x}_{4} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3}+{m}_{4} \\\\  & =2+3+1+5 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the total moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3}+{m}_{4}{x}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 5+5\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =2+9+5+30 \\\\  & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{46}{11}.\n$$"] [17592186129543 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=2,\\,{m}_{2}=3,\\,{m}_{3}=1,\\,{m}_{4}=5.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $x$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=-2,\\,{x}_{3}=1,\\,{x}_{4}=-3.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3}+{m}_{4} \\\\  & =2+3+1+5 \\\\  & =11\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3}+{m}_{4}{x}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =2-6+1-15 \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=-\\frac{18}{11}.\n$$"] [17592186129569 :question/problem "Classify the recurrence relation\n$a_{n} = a_{n - 2} - 2a_{n - 3}, a_{1} = 2, a_{2} = 1, a_{3} = 0, n \\ge 4$.\nThe recurrence relation $a_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}},\nis\n{{field-2}},\nand is\n{{field-3}}."] [17592186129577 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x) = 2\\cot (\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{π}{4})$? Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [17592186129579 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}_{1}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 5 {x}_{1} ( t ) - 7 {x}_{2} ( t ) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 2 {x}_{2} ( t ) .\\end{array} \\right.$"] [17592186129580 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-2\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129581 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, we have $E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, the change of variables that decouples the system is given by $x(t)=Eu(t)$, or equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) &={u}_{1}(t)-{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) &={u}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186129582 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=4\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4(0)}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ 5{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ 5{c}_{2} &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${c}_{2}=2.$ Substituting into the first equation, we obtain ${c}_{1}=1.$ \n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129583 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} &=9 \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2} &=6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}=15\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{1}=5$ into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5+{c}_{2}=6\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =5\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129586 :question/problem "If the following system of differential equations is written in the form $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$, then what is the matrix $A$?\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}_{1}^{\\prime } ( t ) - 4 {x}_{1} ( t ) - 9 {x}_{2} ( t ) = 0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\\prime } ( t ) - 7 {x}_{1} ( t ) + 5 {x}_{2} ( t ) = 0\\end{array} \\right.$"] [17592186129589 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}_{1}^{\\prime } ( t ) - {x}_{1} ( t ) - 5 {x}_{2} ( t ) = 2 \\ln   ( t ) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\\prime } ( t ) + 2 {x}_{2} ( t ) = - {t}^{2}\\end{array} \\right.$\nThe system of linear ODEs above can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n| $x^{′}(t) =$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $x(t) +$ |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |  |"] [17592186129602 :question/problem "$v = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}, A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above.\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $λ = 1$, then what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$?"] [17592186129608 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 10 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -14{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 9 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 10 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 10 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 9 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186129609 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186129611 :question/worked-solution "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz).$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=3.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(e,{e}^{2},-e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz))k \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\cdot 6{x}^{2})i+(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\cdot z)j+(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\cdot y)k \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\text{ }i+\\frac{z}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\text{ }j+\\frac{y}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(e,{e}^{2},-e) & =\\frac{6{e}^{2}}{2{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}(-e)}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(-e)}{2{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}(-e)}\\text{ }j+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}(-e)}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{e}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{e}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{6}{e},\\,-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{e}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨\\frac{6}{e},\\,-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{e}⟩.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{6}{e}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\right)}=-6e.$"] [17592186129616 :question/problem "${∬}_{|x|+|y|\\le 1}\\,\\,{e}^{x+y}\\,dA=$"] [17592186129617 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $y =-x + 1$, $y =-x$, $y = 2x - 2$, and $y = 2x$.\nExpress $∬_(D)f(x, y)dA$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type II regions."] [17592186129620 :question/problem "![](images/23/23e724b9fb4e2d06fad67a0bce75d7ff4555ae1dffdda39442e7dee414ea3c78.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed by the curves $x = y^{3}$, $x = y^{2}$, and the $y$-axis, as shown in the picture. Evaluate $∬_(D)5ydA$."] [17592186129621 :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({2}^{y+1}{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=$"] [17592186129622 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}y}\\,\\text{d}A$, where $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 4\\}.$"] [17592186129623 :question/problem "Evaluate the repeated integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{e^{2x}}{y}dxdy$."] [17592186129624 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =3y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =3x-\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨3y,\\,3x-\\frac{1}{y},\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨3,5,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨3y,\\,3x-\\frac{1}{y},\\,-1⟩=⟨3,5,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3cy &=3 \\\\ 3cx-\\frac{c}{y} &=5 \\\\ -c &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3(1)y=3\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ and $y=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n3(1)x-\\frac{1}{1}=5\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=2$ and $y=1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =3(2)(1)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector  $v$ at $(2,1,6).$"] [17592186129625 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=0$ while the second equation gives $y=0.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$"] [17592186129626 :question/problem "Evaluate \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$"] [17592186129627 :question/problem "![](images/bc/bccbe3973fa63908f5e264f4566e1207c5019dc1de9c36ef42481428527aef3b.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the semicircle shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?"] [17592186129628 :question/problem "If $D = {(x, y): (x - 1)^{2} + y^{2} \\le 1, y \\ge 0}$, then $∬_(D)x\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}dA =$"] [17592186129631 :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{π/3}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}r^{2}\\sin θdrdθ$?"] [17592186129633 :question/problem "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has an infinite discontinuity.\n$f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{ {x}^{2} - 4}} , & x < - 3 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{x + 4}} , & x \\ge  - 3\\end{array} \\right.$"] [17592186129634 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T([\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2u+v) \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186129642 :question/problem "$B = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}, [T]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of $R^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S$."] [17592186129643 :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$u = x - y, v = y$,\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - y)^{2} - y = 0$ and the line $y = 1$."] [17592186129651 :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{-2y + 3}f(x, y)dxdy$."] [17592186129652 :question/problem "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$"] [17592186129653 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{3}+\\pi )\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186129654 :question/problem "Let $v$ be a non-zero vector from $R^{2}$ and let $A$ be the rotation-scaling matrix given by\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}5\\sqrt{2} & -5\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 5\\sqrt{2} & 5\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nThe description of $Av$ in terms of geometric transformations in the plane is given below. What are the missing entries?\n\n> *To compute the vector $Av$, we rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $θ = \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$ and then scale the result by a factor of $r = \\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$.*"] [17592186129658 :question/problem "Find the scale factor of the transformation represented by the rotation-scaling matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & -2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186129660 :question/problem "Which of the following are rotation-scaling matrices?\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $C=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}]$"] [17592186129660 :question/worked-solution "A rotation-scaling matrix has the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers that are not equal to zero simultaneously. Notice that the entries on the main diagonal are the same, while the antidiagonal entries have opposite signs.\n\nWith that in mind let's check each of the given matrices:\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- $B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the antidiagonal do not have opposite signs.\n\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the main diagonal are not the same.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186129661 :question/problem "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has infinite discontinuities.\n\n$f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{x - 9}} , & x \\ne  9 \\\\ 0 , & x = 9\\end{array} \\right.$"] [17592186129663 :question/problem "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A = PCP^{-1}$, what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A$?"] [17592186129675 :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}f(x, y)dydx$."] [17592186129684 :question/problem "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2 \\cdot 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A = PBP^{-1}$, what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A$?"] [17592186129686 :question/problem "Consider the vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2 \\cdot 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2\\textrm{i} - 1 \\\\ -\\textrm{i} \\\\ -2 + 4\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $λ_{1} =-1$ and $λ_{2} =-2 + 3\\textrm{i}$, respectively.\nGiven that $A = PBP^{-1}$, which of the following could be the matrix $P$?"] [17592186129687 :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 7 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B$, where the $2 \\cdot 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A = PBP^{-1}$."] [17592186129700 :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\sqrt{1 - x^{2}}}^{1 - x^{2}}f(x, y)dydx$."] [17592186129702 :question/problem "$∭_(R)f(x, y, z)dV = \\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{b}\\displaystyle \\int_{∗}^{∗}\\displaystyle \\int_{∗}^{∗}f(x, y, z)dzdydx$\nGiven that the triple integral above is considered over the rectangular box\n$R = {(x, y, z): 0 \\le x \\le 2,-2 \\le y \\le 0, 0 \\le z \\le 1}$,\nwhat is the value of $a + b$?"] [17592186129706 :question/problem "Find $B = A^(―)$, where $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 + 2\\textrm{i} & 1 - 2\\textrm{i} & -\\textrm{i} \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1 - 2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 2 + \\textrm{i} & \\textrm{i} & -\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$. What is the value of $b_{12} + b_{33}$?"] [17592186129715 :question/problem "$∭_(R)f(x, y, z)dV = \\displaystyle \\int_{∗}^{∗}\\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{b}\\displaystyle \\int_{∗}^{∗}f(x, y, z)dxdzdy$\nGiven that the triple integral above is considered over the rectangular box\n$R = {(x, y, z): - 2 \\le x \\le 1, 1 \\le y \\le 3, 2 \\le z \\le 4}$,\nwhat is the value of $a + b$?"] [17592186129718 :question/problem "$v = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 3 - 2\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 2 - 5\\textrm{i}$, find a matrix $P$ such that $A = PCP^{-1}$."] [17592186129722 :question/problem "![](images/a0/a0d6d2ed215df7808ebafe50b6676c0502b2aed3bd2d3d8661bd4363155eaa65.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186129770 :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x) = \\frac{3}{2}\\tan (2x + \\frac{π}{4}) + \\frac{1}{2}$? Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [17592186129782 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"] [17592186129782 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period $T$ is given by $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{5}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{6\\pi }{5}.$"] [17592186129824 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x) = 3\\cot (\\frac{x}{4} - \\frac{π}{3})$."] [17592186129825 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x) = \\tan (\\frac{3x}{4} - \\frac{π}{3}) + 2$."] [17592186129843 :question/worked-solution "We notice that:\n\n- the limits for the variable $x$ are from $0$ to $1$, and\n\n- the limits for the variable $y$ are from $1$ to $3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the region of integration is rectangular, we can simply swap the order of integration. This gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{1}^{3}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is ${∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ only."] [17592186129844 :question/problem "Which of the following equalities are true given that\n$D = {(x, y): 1 \\le x \\le \\sqrt{2}, 0 \\le y \\le 1}$.\n\n1. $∬_(D)\\frac{x}{y^{2} + 1}dA = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{x}{y^{2} + 1}dxdy$\n2. $∬_(D)\\frac{x}{y^{2} + 1}dA = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{x}{y^{2} + 1}dydx$\n3. $∬_(D)\\frac{x}{y^{2} + 1}dA = \\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{y^{2} + 1}dydx$"] [17592186129845 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\text{ }{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A$ over the rectangle $R=[0,\\pi ]\\times [0,1].$"] [17592186129846 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the value of the double integral ${∬}_{R}y\\sqrt{xy}\\text{ }\\text{d}A$ over the rectangle $R=[1,4]\\times [0,1]?$"] [17592186129847 :question/problem "Calculate $∬_(R)(1 - 3x^{2} + 2y)dA$, where $R = [- 1, 1] \\cdot [0, 1]$."] [17592186129848 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\frac{1-3x{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[1,e]\\times [2,3].$"] [17592186129849 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$, where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}6x,\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,-1<y<0, \\\\ {e}^{x+y},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,0<y<1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$"] [17592186129850 :question/problem "${\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}ij=$"] [17592186129851 :question/problem "What is $∑_(i = 1)^(3)∑_(j = 2)^(3)(i^{2} + j)$?"] [17592186129852 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $∬_(D)x^{3}ydA$, where\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 2,-2 \\le y \\le 0}$."] [17592186129853 :question/problem "Let $R=[0,4]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(4{x}_{i-1}+2{y}_{j-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(4{x}_{i}+2{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0})\n$$"] [17592186129854 :question/problem "Let $R=[2,5]\\times [-2,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(2{y}_{j-1}-2{x}_{i}+1)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(2{y}_{j}-2{x}_{i-1}+1)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0}) \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}-{x}_{0})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129874 :question/problem "Given that $C$ and $D$ are finite, non-overlapping regions and that\n$∬_(C)f(x, y)dA = 7, ∬_(D)f(x, y)dA = 1$,\nfind the value of\n$∬_(C∪D)5f(x, y)dA$."] [17592186129887 :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd1a5a140c3a2d430312d6f6ceeb21d6b7282119c8ec53365553ff845d8c64d5.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is the semicircle shown above."] [17592186130324 :tutorial/content "Consider the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$ below. We will use this graph to compute $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 4} \\sqrt{x} \\, .$\n\n![](images/e6/e6423b96ce60348e780ffd437207bdbeb456e3aae2888d7525f8c4e680f4ee43.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $4,$ the value of $y$ approaches $\\sqrt{4}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} \\sqrt{x} = \\sqrt{4} = 2.\n$$\n\nThe limit of the square root function at a point is equal to the value of the square root function at that point, and this is true for any point *that is greater than $0.$* However, for points that are less than or equal to $0,$ the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c}\\sqrt{x}=\\begin{cases}\\sqrt{c} & \\text{if }c>0\\\\\\text{DNE} & \\text{if }c\\leq0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nWhy doesn't the limit exist for $c \\leq 0$?\n\n- For $c < 0,$ the limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, c} \\sqrt{x}$ does not exist because the square root function $f(x) = \\sqrt{x}$ is not defined for negative inputs.\n\n- Although $\\sqrt{x}$ is defined at $x=0,$ the left limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0^-} \\sqrt{x}$ does not exist because $\\sqrt{x}$ is undefined left of $x=0.$ Consequently, the overall limit $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} \\sqrt{x}$ does not exist either."] [17592186130328 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look at the function $y=f(x)$, plotted below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c1e716fb1b2a70d8ee9b74f13cf81e72bb63a147db5a5637a890bf1166b5e87.png)\n\nThe function has a discontinuity at $x=1$, but can we classify it?\n\nNotice that when we check our continuity conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(1) = 3$, so $f(1)$ exists $\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 1} f(x) = 2$, so $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 1} f(x)$ exists $\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 1} f(x) \\neq f(1)$ $\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo the first two conditions passed, yet the third failed. When this happens, we have a **point discontinuity**. We also call this a **removable discontinuity**.\n\nIn general, a function $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=c$ if \n\n- $f(c)$ exists $\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to c} f(x)$ exists $\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to c} f(x) \\neq f(c)$$\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nIn addition, both $f(c)$ and $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ should be finite."] [17592186130355 :tutorial/content "Suppose $A$ is a real $2\\!\\times\\! 2$ matrix. To interpret the rotation-scaling form $A=PCP^{-1},$ we should bear in mind the following:\n\n- The matrix $C$ rotates vectors $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^2$ and then scales them.\n\n- We can think of $P$ as representing the following change of variable:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = P\\mathbf u \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad  \\mathbf u = P^{-1}\\mathbf x\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ can be thought of as a change of variable from $\\mathbf x$ to $\\mathbf u,$ followed by a rotation and scaling before returning to the original variable.\n\n![](images/ab/ab40869137296cea63fe209a842a4713fcae4fbf53c5089e6722b70bdf3f3230.png)\n\nFor example, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\sqrt{3} + 1 & - 2 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{3} - 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThis is a real matrix with complex eigenvalues and is thus similar to a rotation-scaling matrix. \n\nUsing the techniques described in this lesson, we can show that the rotation-scaling decomposition of $A$ is\n\n$$\nA\n=\n\\underbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n}_{P}\n\\cdot \n\\underbrace{\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\sqrt{3} & - 1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{array} \\right]}\n}_{C}\n\\cdot\n\\underbrace{\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}^{-1}\n}_{P^{-1}}.\n$$\nThe matrix $C$ can be written explicitly as a constant multiple of a rotation matrix as follows:\n$$\nA\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n\\cdot\n\\underbrace{\\left( {\\color{blue}{2}} \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} & - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\\end{array} \\right]} \\right)}_{C}\n\\cdot\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & - 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}^{-1}\n$$\n\nThus, the matrix $C$ represents the following:\n\n- a rotation of $\\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac\\pi 6$ counterclockwise about $O,$ and\n\n- an enlargement of scale factor ${\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\nThe image of a unit square centered at the origin under the action of $A$ can therefore be decomposed as follows:\n\n![](images/92/92f2ab84b9ac8f36a93c42bf93b70b08a3e64eb3920a8bd2d5276217d936ab79.png)"] [17592186130390 :tutorial/content "We can express $\\rho,$ $\\theta,$ and $\\phi$ in terms of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ using the following relations:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}, \\qquad\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad\n\\cos\\phi = \\dfrac{z}{\\rho}.\n$$\n\nTo help to understand where these formulas come from, note that:\n\n- The formula for $\\rho$ is given by the distance of the point $(x,y,z)$ from the origin.\n\n- Since the angle $\\theta$ corresponds to the same polar angle that we see in plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the relationship between $x,y,$ and $\\theta$ in spherical polar coordinates is the same as for plane polar coordinates.\n\n- The formula for $\\phi$ is simply a rearrangement of $z = \\rho\\cos\\phi.$\n\nLet's again consider our point $P$ with Cartesian coordinates $(1,\\sqrt{3}, 2),$ and convert the coordinates into spherical polar coordinates.\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since  $(x,y) = (1,\\sqrt{3})$ lies in the first quadrant, we can compute the corresponding angle $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $\\phi$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho }\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}\\left(\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(2\\sqrt2, \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{\\pi}4 \\right).$"] [17592186130415 :tutorial/content "Our task is to find the quotient and remainder of the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^2+8x-5}{x+5}\n$$\n\nBefore we start, we need to check that the polynomials are written in standard form. In this case, the terms in both the numerator and denominator are in descending order of their degree, so we're ready to begin.\n\nTo carry out the division, we follow the following step-by-step process.\n\n **Step 1** \n\nWe start by writing the dividend and the divisor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n **Step 2** \n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}2x^2})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}x}).$ This gives $\\left(\\dfrac{{\\color{red}2x^2}}{{\\color{blue}x}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}2x}.$ We write this result in the quotient line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the ${\\color{#8b008b}{2x}}$ we just found by the full divisor ${{\\color{blue}{x}}+5}.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}{2x}}{({\\color{blue}{x}}+5)}={2x^2+10x}.$ We place this result underneath the dividend:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract ${2x^2+10x}$ from ${2x^2+8x - 5}$ and get ${{-2x-5}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3** \n\nWe now repeat the process. The dividend is now ${{\\color{red}{-2x}}-5}.$\n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}-2x})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}x}).$ This gives $\\left(\\dfrac{{\\color{red}-2x}}{{\\color{blue}x}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}{-2}}.$ We write this result in the quotient line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x\\,\\,-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the ${\\color{#8b008b}{-2}}$ term we just found by the full divisor ${{\\color{blue}{x}}+5}.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}{-2}}({{\\color{blue}{x}}+5})={-2x-10}.$ We place this result underneath the dividend:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x\\,\\,-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-2x-10}_{―}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract ${{-2x-10}}$ from ${{-2x-5}}$ to get ${\\color{red}{5}}{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x\\,\\,-2 \\\\  & x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{1}8x-\\phantom{1}5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-5 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-2x-10}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're left with the constant ${\\color{red}{5}}.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n- the quotient of the polynomial division is $\\color{blue}{2x-2},$ and\n\n- the remainder is ${\\color{red}{5}}.$"] [17592186130433 :tutorial/content "When multiplying real numbers, it's usually possible to say whether the product is rational or irrational.\n\nWe have three possible cases:\n\n- The product of two rational numbers is *always* rational since the result must be a fraction of the form $\\dfrac ab$, where $a$ and $b$ are integers.\n- The product of a *nonzero* rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational. To demonstrate, let's consider the following example:\n$2\\cdot \\sqrt 2$\nIt's easy to see that this is irrational. Writing down the product in decimal form, we get\n$2 \\cdot 1.414213562… = 2.828427125…$\nwhich is irrational since the numbers after the decimal point extend forever with no regular pattern.\n- The product of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational! For example, the following product is irrational:\n$\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{3}|= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 3}; = \\sqrt{6}$\nHowever, the following product is rational:\n$\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}|= \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 2}; = \\sqrt{2^{2}}; = 2$"] [17592186130447 :tutorial/content "Rational expressions are closed when raised to *integer* powers since these can be reduced to repeated multiplication.\n\nFor example, let $S$ be a rational expression. Then:\n\n- $S^3 = S \\cdot S \\cdot S$ is a rational expression.\n- $S^{-2} = \\dfrac{1}{S^2} = \\dfrac{1}{S \\cdot S}$ is a rational expression provided $S$ is not identical to zero.\n\nHowever, rational expressions are *not* closed when raised to *non-integer* powers.\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\nS^{1/2} = \\sqrt{S}\n$$\n\nis *not* rational for all rational $S$ because it contains a square root. For example, if\n\n$$\nS = \\dfrac1x\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nS^{1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt x}\n$$\n\nwhich is not rational.\n\nIt's worth noting that $S^{1/2}$ *is* rational for *some* choices of $S$. For example, if we let\n\n$$\nS = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nS^{3/2} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)^{1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nwhich *is* rational. However, since at least one rational expression exists such that $S^{1/2}$ is not rational, the rational expressions are *not* closed under the square root operation.\n\nIn general, applying exponential, logarithmic, and trigonometric operations to a rational expression does not result in a rational expression. Let's see an example."] [17592186130479 :tutorial/content "When a collection of masses is distributed in two or more dimensions of space, we must take moments about a fixed **axis.**\n\nIn the following work, we will only consider moments about the coordinate axes. \n\nConsider the collection of point masses below, where the distances along the $x$- and $y$-axes are measured in meters. \n\n![](images/32/32f9c207b835469dba729e3d5745dd1225eed6153aac4517e2c40e61b7edb126.png)\n\nThe center of mass of this two-dimensional system, denoted $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y}),$ is the point where the entire mass of the system could be concentrated to give the same total moment about the $x$- and $y$-axes as the original system. \n\nMore precisely, the center of mass of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $(x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2),\\ldots,(x_n,y_n)$ in the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left(\\dfrac{M_y}{m},\\dfrac{M_x}{m}\\right),\n$$\n\n \nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_x = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i y_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_y = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nLet's compute $\\bar{x},$ the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass for this system. \n\nFirst, we denote the masses as\n\n$$\nm_1 = 3, \\qquad m_2 = 5, \\qquad m_3 = 4.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $x$-coordinates of these masses are\n\n$$\nx_1 = -2, \\qquad x_2 = -1, \\qquad x_3 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =3+5+4 \\\\  & =12\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $\\boldsymbol y$**-axis** is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-6-5+16 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_y}{m} = \\dfrac{5}{12}.\n$$"] [17592186133482 :question/problem "![](images/fc/fc19dd4415663ab027e0fc386d4c5bc81a28c1bc26d5f6cf2083f4f4228a4dea.png)\n\n \n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 12z^2 \\: \\mathrm{d}V$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [17592186133485 :question/problem "![](images/b0/b0817c04ee57a8352f8f56a4c232668d415c1573e37a81729752d65c0fb9e1c1.png)\n\n \n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 36z^2  \\: \\mathrm{d}V$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the right one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [17592186133558 :question/worked-solution "First, we move the radicals to separate sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2x + 5} - \\sqrt{x + 6} &= 1 \\\\\n\\sqrt{2x + 5} &= \\sqrt{x + 6} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we raise both sides to the power of $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2x + 5} &= \\sqrt{x + 6} + 1 \\\\\n(\\sqrt{2x + 5})^{2} &= (\\sqrt{x + 6} + 1)^{2} \\\\\n2x + 5 &= (\\sqrt{x + 6})^{2} + 2\\sqrt{x + 6} + 1^{2} \\\\\n2x + 5 &= x + 7 + 2\\sqrt{x + 6} \\\\\n2\\sqrt{x + 6} &= 2x + 5 - x - 7 \\\\\n2\\sqrt{x + 6} &= x - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we have an equation with one square root on one side and a linear expression on the other side. To solve this type of equation, we raise both sides to the power of $2$ one more time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2\\sqrt{x + 6})^{2} &= (x - 2)^{2} \\\\\n4(x + 6) &= x^{2} - 4x + 4 \\\\\n4x + 24 &= x^{2} - 4x + 4 \\\\\nx^{2} - 8x - 20 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 10x + 2x - 20 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 10)(x + 2) &= 0 \\\\\n&= x = 10,-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we check the obtained values by substituting them into the original equation.\n\n- Checking $x=10$, we get\n$\\sqrt{2(10) + 5}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{(10) + 6} + 1; \\sqrt{25}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{16} + 1; 5|= ^(?)4 + 1; 5|= 5.✓$\nSo, the value $x=10$ is indeed a solution of the original equation\n- Checking $x=-2$, we get\n$\\sqrt{2(-2) + 5}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{(-2) + 6} + 1; \\sqrt{1} = 1 \\mid \\ne 2 + 1 = 3.\\cdot$\nThe value $x=-2$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt {2x +5} -\\sqrt {x+6} = 1\n$$\n\nhas a single solution $x=10$."] [17592186133562 :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r, \\theta) = \\left(7, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(-\\dfrac 72, -\\dfrac{7\\sqrt 3}2\\right).$"] [17592186133580 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 5\\end{array} \\right]}, \\mathbf{v}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 3 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}$ and $\\mathbf{w}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}$ be vectors such that $\\mathbf{u} \\!\\perp\\! \\mathbf{w}$. Find $(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})\\cdot \\mathbf{w} + \\mathbf{u}\\cdot(\\mathbf{v}+ \\mathbf{w}).$"] [17592186133581 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 8 \\\\ a \\\\ b \\\\ - 10\\end{array} \\right]}, \\mathbf{v}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}6 \\\\ - 15 \\\\ 9 \\\\ - 12\\end{array} \\right]},$ and $\\mathbf{w}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ - 5 \\\\ - 3\\end{array} \\right]}$ be vectors such that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$. What is the value of $a+b?$"] [17592186133587 :question/problem "For the vector $\\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{array} \\right]},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $H=\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 6 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 3 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}$\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $V=\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{6}}\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}$"] [17592186133588 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{array} \\right]}$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $W=\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]},{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}\\right\\},$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a+c}{b}.$"] [17592186133589 :question/problem "Find $H^{\\perp}$ if $H=\\textrm{Span}\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]},{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}.$"] [17592186133602 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\mathbf{a}_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set $\\mathbf{v}_1=\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}=\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{- \\frac{7}{3}} \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\frac{13}{3}} \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\frac{10}{3}}\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\dfrac 7 3,$ $c=\\dfrac{10}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{c} = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{10}{3}\\right)} = -\\dfrac{7}{10}.\n$$"] [17592186133603 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]} \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\mathbf{a}_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 5 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ - 3\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set $\\mathbf{v}_1=\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-5)\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\} = \\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{- 4} \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{- 3} \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{- 1}\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\textrm{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=-4,$ $c=-1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{a} = \\dfrac{-1}{-4} = \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$"] [17592186133604 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]} \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\mathbf{a}_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set $\\mathbf{v}_1=\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{6\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}_1=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{v}_1}{\\Vert\\mathbf{v}_1\\Vert}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2^2+2^2+(-1)^2}}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]} ={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} \\\\ - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}}\\end{array} \\right]}$\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}_2=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{v}_2}{\\Vert\\mathbf{v}_2\\Vert}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{4^2+(-2)^2+4^2}}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]} ={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} \\\\ - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}}\\end{array} \\right]}$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{u}_1, \\mathbf{u}_2 \\} = \\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{3}\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{- \\frac{1}{3}} \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\frac{2}{3}}\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}$ is an orthonormal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=\\dfrac{2}{3},$ $b=-\\dfrac1  3,$ $c=\\dfrac{2}{3},$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c| = \\left| \\dfrac{2}{3} \\right| + \\left|-\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right| + \\left| \\dfrac{2}{3} \\right| = \\dfrac 5 3.\n$$"] [17592186133605 :question/worked-solution "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: We set $\\mathbf{v}_1=\\mathbf{a}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n**Step 2**: We set $\\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{a}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_3$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf{v}_3$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\left(-\\frac{2}{4}\\right)\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{0}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\dfrac 1 2,$ $c=-\\dfrac 3 2,$ and $\\dfrac{c}{a} = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{\\left(-\\dfrac 1 2\\right)} = 3.$"] [17592186133606 :question/worked-solution "We find an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal B$ of $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set $\\mathbf{v}_1=\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_3$ orthogonal to $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\left(-\\frac{3}{3}\\right)\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $b=\\dfrac 3 2,$ $c=-\\dfrac 3 2,$ and $\\dfrac{b}{c} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}=-1.$"] [17592186133607 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set $\\mathbf{v}_1 = \\mathbf{a}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{12}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & 2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large A}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}} & - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}}\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large Q}\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\sqrt{6} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{2 \\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $q_{11} \\cdot r_{12} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right) \\cdot \\left({2\\sqrt6}\\right) = 2.$"] [17592186133632 :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\textrm{Span}\\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 2 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]},{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} \\right\\}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to $H^{\\perp}?$\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\\qquad \n\\mathbf{v}_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\\qquad \n\\mathbf{v}_3={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 6\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$"] [17592186133643 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ given that $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y \\,\\mathbf i + (x+y)\\,\\mathbf j$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf r(t) = (1+2t)\\,\\mathbf{i} + (1-t)\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0, 1].$"] [17592186133644 :question/problem "Find an integral that is equivalent to $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C xy^3 \\, \\textrm{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf r(t)= \\left \\langle \\cos t, \\sin t \\right\\rangle$ for $t \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$"] [17592186133645 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C}xy\\,\\textrm{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t) = 3t\\,\\mathbf{i}+4t\\,\\mathbf{j}\\,$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 1.$"] [17592186133646 :question/problem "Find a definite integral that's equivalent to $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ given that $\\mathbf F(x, y) = xy \\,\\mathbf i - x^2\\,\\mathbf j$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf r(t) = t^3\\,\\mathbf{i} + t^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0, 1].$"] [17592186133648 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $x=0,$ $y=0,$ and the curve $y=\\sqrt{4 - x^2}.$ What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?"] [17592186133649 :question/problem "If $D$ is the finite region in the first quadrant enclosed by the curves $y=3x$ and $y=x^3,$ then what is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} f\\left( {x,y} \\right) \\, \\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?"] [17592186133650 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\int_0^x x^2y \\,\\textrm{d}y \\,\\textrm{d}x.$"] [17592186133651 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} xy\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\:$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region below the $x$-axis enclosed by the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 4.$"] [17592186133653 :question/problem "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ and $y=1-x$ from $y=0$ to $y=1,$ as shown below, then what is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x^2y+xy^2) \\, \\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?\n\n![](images/0e/0e4cf57408f9ca18dc4613317b8b9bc8b3149da0bf273ad03a8a8a48a6e6f454.png)"] [17592186133654 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=3-2x$ below the line $y=3.$ What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} f\\left( {x,y} \\right) \\, \\mathrm{d}A\\:$ expressed as a repeated integral?"] [17592186133655 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{\\sqrt{\\pi}}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\int_0^{y^2} \\frac{\\sin y^2}{y} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\,\\mathrm{d}y.$"] [17592186133656 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\:$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves $x^2+y^2=4$ and $x=0,$ and above the line $y=-1,$ as shown in the picture.\n\n![](images/f9/f93aeee59ad97fde0efceff342e32a19062187261abec865616bfa963f1084a6.png)"] [17592186133677 :question/worked-solution "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**: We set $\\mathbf{v}_1=\\mathbf{a}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n**Step 2**: We set $\\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{a}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_3$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf{v}_3$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{10}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{20}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize $\\mathbf{v}_3$ dividing it by $\\|\\mathbf{v}_3\\| = \\sqrt{10}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $|\\,d\\,| = \\left|-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\right|=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}.$"] [17592186133694 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy^2+ 2xz.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\mathbf r(t)= \\langle e^{t}, t, e^{-t}\\rangle$ for $t\\in \\left[0,1\\right],$ evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r.$"] [17592186133695 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t,  1-t \\right\\rangle, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int _C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r}$ for $\\mathbf F = ye^{xy}\\,\\mathbf i + xe^{xy}\\,\\mathbf j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.*"] [17592186133699 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C} 4x^2y \\, \\mathrm{d}x+ 2y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y},$ where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(1,2)$ and $(0,2),$ traversed counterclockwise."] [17592186133700 :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,1)$ and $(-1,1),$ traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+ (x^2\\cos{y}-y^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}y}$ as a repeated integral."] [17592186133702 :question/problem "![](images/83/83bd636f3a294c641e171c5a8281aaa7068e1931a06229064314e8f96efd0c3a.png)\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} (x^2-3y)\\, \\mathrm{d}x + ( y^2+x)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above."]}
